.TE
.sp
\fBThere are more trivial options for Hungarian Notation:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
\fBHungarianNotation.General.*\fP
Options are not belonging to any specific Decl.
.TP
\fBHungarianNotation.CString.*\fP
Options for NULL\-terminated string.
.TP
\fBHungarianNotation.DerivedType.*\fP
Options for derived types.
.TP
\fBHungarianNotation.PrimitiveType.*\fP
Options for primitive types.
.TP
\fBHungarianNotation.UserDefinedType.*\fP
Options for user\-defined types.
.UNINDENT
.SS Options for Hungarian Notation
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBHungarianNotation.General.TreatStructAsClass\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBHungarianNotation.DerivedType.Array\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBHungarianNotation.DerivedType.Pointer\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBHungarianNotation.DerivedType.FunctionPointer\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBHungarianNotation.CString.CharPointer\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBHungarianNotation.CString.CharArray\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBHungarianNotation.CString.WideCharPointer\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBHungarianNotation.CString.WideCharArray\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBHungarianNotation.PrimitiveType.*\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBHungarianNotation.UserDefinedType.*\fP
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B HungarianNotation.General.TreatStructAsClass
When defined, the check will treat naming of struct as a class.
The default value is \fIfalse\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B HungarianNotation.DerivedType.Array
When defined, the check will ensure variable name will add the prefix with
the given string. The default prefix is \fIa\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B HungarianNotation.DerivedType.Pointer
When defined, the check will ensure variable name will add the prefix with
the given string. The default prefix is \fIp\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B HungarianNotation.DerivedType.FunctionPointer
When defined, the check will ensure variable name will add the prefix with
the given string. The default prefix is \fIfn\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Before:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
// Array
int DataArray[2] = {0};

// Pointer
void *DataBuffer = NULL;

// FunctionPointer
typedef void (*FUNC_PTR)();
FUNC_PTR FuncPtr = NULL;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
After:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
// Array
int aDataArray[2] = {0};

// Pointer
void *pDataBuffer = NULL;

// FunctionPointer
typedef void (*FUNC_PTR)();
FUNC_PTR fnFuncPtr = NULL;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B HungarianNotation.CString.CharPointer
When defined, the check will ensure variable name will add the prefix with
the given string. The default prefix is \fIsz\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B HungarianNotation.CString.CharArray
When defined, the check will ensure variable name will add the prefix with
the given string. The default prefix is \fIsz\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B HungarianNotation.CString.WideCharPointer
When defined, the check will ensure variable name will add the prefix with
the given string. The default prefix is \fIwsz\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B HungarianNotation.CString.WideCharArray
When defined, the check will ensure variable name will add the prefix with
the given string. The default prefix is \fIwsz\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Before:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
// CharPointer
const char *NamePtr = \(dqName\(dq;

// CharArray
const char NameArray[] = \(dqName\(dq;

// WideCharPointer
const wchar_t *WideNamePtr = L\(dqName\(dq;

// WideCharArray
const wchar_t WideNameArray[] = L\(dqName\(dq;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
After:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
// CharPointer
const char *szNamePtr = \(dqName\(dq;

// CharArray
const char szNameArray[] = \(dqName\(dq;

// WideCharPointer
const wchar_t *wszWideNamePtr = L\(dqName\(dq;

// WideCharArray
const wchar_t wszWideNameArray[] = L\(dqName\(dq;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B HungarianNotation.PrimitiveType.*
When defined, the check will ensure variable name of involved primitive
types will add the prefix with the given string. The default prefixes are
defined in the default mapping table.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B HungarianNotation.UserDefinedType.*
When defined, the check will ensure variable name of involved primitive
types will add the prefix with the given string. The default prefixes are
defined in the default mapping table.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Before:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
int8_t   ValueI8      = 0;
int16_t  ValueI16     = 0;
int32_t  ValueI32     = 0;
int64_t  ValueI64     = 0;
uint8_t  ValueU8      = 0;
uint16_t ValueU16     = 0;
uint32_t ValueU32     = 0;
uint64_t ValueU64     = 0;
float    ValueFloat   = 0.0;
double   ValueDouble  = 0.0;
ULONG    ValueUlong   = 0;
DWORD    ValueDword   = 0;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
After:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
int8_t   i8ValueI8    = 0;
int16_t  i16ValueI16  = 0;
int32_t  i32ValueI32  = 0;
int64_t  i64ValueI64  = 0;
uint8_t  u8ValueU8    = 0;
uint16_t u16ValueU16  = 0;
uint32_t u32ValueU32  = 0;
uint64_t u64ValueU64  = 0;
float    fValueFloat  = 0.0;
double   dValueDouble = 0.0;
ULONG    ulValueUlong = 0;
DWORD    dwValueDword = 0;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-implicit\-bool\-cast
.sp
This check has been renamed to readability\-implicit\-bool\-conversion \%<>\&.
.SS readability\-implicit\-bool\-conversion
.sp
This check can be used to find implicit conversions between built\-in types and
booleans. Depending on use case, it may simply help with readability of the
code, or in some cases, point to potential bugs which remain unnoticed due to
implicit conversions.
.sp
The following is a real\-world example of bug which was hiding behind implicit
\fBbool\fP conversion:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
class Foo {
  int m_foo;

public:
  void setFoo(bool foo) { m_foo = foo; } // warning: implicit conversion bool \-> int
  int getFoo() { return m_foo; }
};

void use(Foo& foo) {
  bool value = foo.getFoo(); // warning: implicit conversion int \-> bool
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This code is the result of unsuccessful refactoring, where type of \fBm_foo\fP
changed from \fBbool\fP to \fBint\fP\&. The programmer forgot to change all
occurrences of \fBbool\fP, and the remaining code is no longer correct, yet it
still compiles without any visible warnings.
.sp
In addition to issuing warnings, fix\-it hints are provided to help solve the
reported issues. This can be used for improving readability of code, for
example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
void conversionsToBool() {
  float floating;
  bool boolean = floating;
  // ^ propose replacement: bool boolean = floating != 0.0f;

  int integer;
  if (integer) {}
  // ^ propose replacement: if (integer != 0) {}

  int* pointer;
  if (!pointer) {}
  // ^ propose replacement: if (pointer == nullptr) {}

  while (1) {}
  // ^ propose replacement: while (true) {}
}

void functionTakingInt(int param);

void conversionsFromBool() {
  bool boolean;
  functionTakingInt(boolean);
  // ^ propose replacement: functionTakingInt(static_cast<int>(boolean));

  functionTakingInt(true);
  // ^ propose replacement: functionTakingInt(1);
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In general, the following conversion types are checked:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
integer expression/literal to boolean (conversion from a single bit bitfield
to boolean is explicitly allowed, since there\(aqs no ambiguity / information
loss in this case),
.IP \(bu 2
floating expression/literal to boolean,
.IP \(bu 2
pointer/pointer to member/\fBnullptr\fP/\fBNULL\fP to boolean,
.IP \(bu 2
boolean expression/literal to integer (conversion from boolean to a single
bit bitfield is explicitly allowed),
.IP \(bu 2
boolean expression/literal to floating.
.UNINDENT
.sp
The rules for generating fix\-it hints are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
in case of conversions from other built\-in type to bool, an explicit
comparison is proposed to make it clear what exactly is being compared:
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbool boolean = floating;\fP is changed to
\fBbool boolean = floating == 0.0f;\fP,
.IP \(bu 2
for other types, appropriate literals are used (\fB0\fP, \fB0u\fP, \fB0.0f\fP,
\fB0.0\fP, \fBnullptr\fP),
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
in case of negated expressions conversion to bool, the proposed replacement
with comparison is simplified:
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBif (!pointer)\fP is changed to \fBif (pointer == nullptr)\fP,
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
in case of conversions from bool to other built\-in types, an explicit
\fBstatic_cast\fP (or a C\-style cast since C23) is proposed to make it clear
that a conversion is taking place:
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBint integer = boolean;\fP is changed to
\fBint integer = static_cast<int>(boolean);\fP,
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
if the conversion is performed on type literals, an equivalent literal is
proposed, according to what type is actually expected, for example:
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfunctionTakingBool(0);\fP is changed to \fBfunctionTakingBool(false);\fP,
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfunctionTakingInt(true);\fP is changed to \fBfunctionTakingInt(1);\fP,
.IP \(bu 2
for other types, appropriate literals are used (\fBfalse\fP, \fBtrue\fP, \fB0\fP,
\fB1\fP, \fB0u\fP, \fB1u\fP, \fB0.0f\fP, \fB1.0f\fP, \fB0.0\fP, \fB1.0f\fP).
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Some additional accommodations are made for pre\-C++11 dialects:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfalse\fP literal conversion to pointer is detected,
.IP \(bu 2
instead of \fBnullptr\fP literal, \fB0\fP is proposed as replacement.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Some additional accommodations are made for C:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbool\fP (or \fB_Bool\fP) operands in logical operators (\fB&&\fP, \fB||\fP) are
ignored.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Occurrences of implicit conversions inside macros and template instantiations
are deliberately ignored, as it is not clear how to deal with such cases.
.SS Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B AllowIntegerConditions
When \fItrue\fP, the check will allow conditional integer conversions. Default
is \fIfalse\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B AllowPointerConditions
When \fItrue\fP, the check will allow conditional pointer conversions. Default
is \fIfalse\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B UseUpperCaseLiteralSuffix
When \fItrue\fP, the replacements will use an uppercase literal suffix in the
provided fixes. Default is \fIfalse\fP\&.
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
Example
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
uint32_t foo;
if (foo) {}
// ^ propose replacement default: if (foo != 0u) {}
// ^ propose replacement with option \(gaUseUpperCaseLiteralSuffix\(ga: if (foo != 0U) {}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-inconsistent\-declaration\-parameter\-name
.sp
Find function declarations which differ in parameter names.
.sp
Example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
// in foo.hpp:
void foo(int a, int b, int c);

// in foo.cpp:
void foo(int d, int e, int f); // warning
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This check should help to enforce consistency in large projects, where it often
happens that a definition of function is refactored, changing the parameter
names, but its declaration in header file is not updated. With this check, we
can easily find and correct such inconsistencies, keeping declaration and
definition always in sync.
.sp
Unnamed parameters are allowed and are not taken into account when comparing
function declarations, for example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
void foo(int a);
void foo(int); // no warning
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
One name is also allowed to be a case\-insensitive prefix/suffix of the other:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
void foo(int count);
void foo(int count_input) { // no warning
  int count = adjustCount(count_input);
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
To help with refactoring, in some cases fix\-it hints are generated to align
parameter names to a single naming convention. This works with the assumption
that the function definition is the most up\-to\-date version, as it directly
references parameter names in its body. Example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
void foo(int a); // warning and fix\-it hint (replace \(dqa\(dq to \(dqb\(dq)
int foo(int b) { return b + 2; } // definition with use of \(dqb\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the case of multiple redeclarations or function template specializations,
a warning is issued for every redeclaration or specialization inconsistent with
the definition or the first declaration seen in a translation unit.
.SS Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B IgnoreMacros
If this option is set to \fItrue\fP (default is \fItrue\fP), the check will not warn
about names declared inside macros.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B Strict
If this option is set to \fItrue\fP (default is \fIfalse\fP), then names must match
exactly (or be absent).
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-inconsistent\-ifelse\-braces
.sp
Detects \fBif\fP/\fBelse\fP statements where one branch uses braces and the other
does not.
.sp
Before:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
if (condition) {
  statement;
} else
  statement;

if (condition)
  statement;

if (condition)
  statement;
else
  statement;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
After:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
if (condition) {
  statement;
} else {
  statement;
}

if (condition)
  statement;

if (condition)
  statement;
else
  statement;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-isolate\-declaration
.sp
Detects local variable declarations declaring more than one variable and
tries to refactor the code to one statement per declaration.
.sp
The automatic code\-transformation will use the same indentation as the original
for every created statement and add a line break after each statement.
It keeps the order of the variable declarations consistent, too.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
void f() {
  int * pointer = nullptr, value = 42, * const const_ptr = &value;
  // This declaration will be diagnosed and transformed into:
  // int * pointer = nullptr;
  // int value = 42;
  // int * const const_ptr = &value;
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The check excludes places where it is necessary or common to declare
multiple variables in one statement and there is no other way supported in the
language. Please note that structured bindings are not considered.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
// It is not possible to transform this declaration and doing the declaration
// before the loop will increase the scope of the variable \(aqBegin\(aq and \(aqEnd\(aq
// which is undesirable.
for (int Begin = 0, End = 100; Begin < End; ++Begin);
if (int Begin = 42, Result = some_function(Begin); Begin == Result);

// It is not possible to transform this declaration because the result is
// not functionality preserving as \(aqj\(aq and \(aqk\(aq would not be part of the
// \(aqif\(aq statement anymore.
if (SomeCondition())
  int i = 42, j = 43, k = function(i,j);
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Limitations
.sp
Global variables and member variables are excluded.
.sp
The check currently does not support the automatic transformation of
member\-pointer\-types.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
struct S {
  int a;
  const int b;
  void f() {}
};

void f() {
  // Only a diagnostic message is emitted
  int S::*p = &S::a, S::*const q = &S::a;
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Furthermore, the transformation is very cautious when it detects various kinds
of macros or preprocessor directives in the range of the statement. In this
case the transformation will not happen to avoid unexpected side\-effects due to
macros.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#define NULL 0
#define MY_NICE_TYPE int **
#define VAR_NAME(name) name##__LINE__
#define A_BUNCH_OF_VARIABLES int m1 = 42, m2 = 43, m3 = 44;

void macros() {
  int *p1 = NULL, *p2 = NULL;
  // Will be transformed to
  // int *p1 = NULL;
  // int *p2 = NULL;

  MY_NICE_TYPE p3, v1, v2;
  // Won\(aqt be transformed, but a diagnostic is emitted.

  int VAR_NAME(v3),
      VAR_NAME(v4),
      VAR_NAME(v5);
  // Won\(aqt be transformed, but a diagnostic is emitted.

  A_BUNCH_OF_VARIABLES
  // Won\(aqt be transformed, but a diagnostic is emitted.

  int Unconditional,
#if CONFIGURATION
      IfConfigured = 42,
#else
      IfConfigured = 0;
#endif
  // Won\(aqt be transformed, but a diagnostic is emitted.
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-magic\-numbers
.sp
Detects magic numbers, integer or floating point literals that are embedded in
code and not introduced via constants or symbols.
.sp
Many coding guidelines advise replacing the magic values with symbolic
constants to improve readability. Here are a few references:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Rule ES.45: Avoid \(dqmagic constants\(dq; use symbolic constants in C++ Core Guidelines \%<https://\:isocpp\:.github\:.io/\:CppCoreGuidelines/\:CppCoreGuidelines#\:res-magic>
.IP \(bu 2
Rule 5.1.1 Use symbolic names instead of literal values in code in High Integrity C++ \%<https://\:www\:.perforce\:.com/\:resources/\:qac/\:high-integrity-cpp-coding-standard-expressions>
.IP \(bu 2
Item 17 in \(dqC++ Coding Standards: 101 Rules, Guidelines and Best
Practices\(dq by Herb Sutter and Andrei Alexandrescu
.IP \(bu 2
Chapter 17 in \(dqClean Code \- A handbook of agile software craftsmanship.\(dq
by Robert C. Martin
.IP \(bu 2
Rule 20701 in \(dqTRAIN REAL TIME DATA PROTOCOL Coding Rules\(dq by Armin\-Hagen
Weiss, Bombardier
.IP \(bu 2
\%<http://\:wiki\:.c2\:.com/?\:MagicNumber>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Examples of magic values:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
template<typename T, size_t N>
struct CustomType {
   T arr[N];
};

struct OtherType {
   CustomType<int, 30> container;
}
CustomType<int, 30> values;

double circleArea = 3.1415926535 * radius * radius;

double totalCharge = 1.08 * itemPrice;

int getAnswer() {
   return \-3; // FILENOTFOUND
}

for (int mm = 1; mm <= 12; ++mm) {
   std::cout << month[mm] << \(aq\en\(aq;
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Example with magic values refactored:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
template<typename T, size_t N>
struct CustomType {
   T arr[N];
};

const size_t NUMBER_OF_ELEMENTS = 30;
using containerType = CustomType<int, NUMBER_OF_ELEMENTS>;

struct OtherType {
   containerType container;
}
containerType values;

double circleArea = M_PI * radius * radius;

const double TAX_RATE = 0.08;  // or make it variable and read from a file

double totalCharge = (1.0 + TAX_RATE) * itemPrice;

int getAnswer() {
   return E_FILE_NOT_FOUND;
}

for (int mm = 1; mm <= MONTHS_IN_A_YEAR; ++mm) {
   std::cout << month[mm] << \(aq\en\(aq;
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For integral literals by default only \fI0\fP and \fI1\fP (and \fI\-1\fP) integer values
are accepted without a warning. This can be overridden with the
\fBIgnoredIntegerValues\fP option. Negative values are accepted if their
absolute value is present in the \fBIgnoredIntegerValues\fP list.
.sp
As a special case for integral values, all powers of two can be accepted
without warning by enabling the \fBIgnorePowersOf2IntegerValues\fP option.
.sp
For floating point literals by default the \fI0.0\fP floating point value is
accepted without a warning. The set of ignored floating point literals can
be configured using the \fBIgnoredFloatingPointValues\fP option.
For each value in that set, the given string value is converted to a
floating\-point value representation used by the target architecture. If a
floating\-point literal value compares equal to one of the converted values,
then that literal is not diagnosed by this check. Because floating\-point
equality is used to determine whether to diagnose or not, the user needs to
be aware of the details of floating\-point representations for any values that
cannot be precisely represented for their target architecture.
.sp
For each value in the \fBIgnoredFloatingPointValues\fP set, both the
single\-precision form and double\-precision form are accepted (for example, if
3.14 is in the set, neither 3.14f nor 3.14 will produce a warning).
.sp
Scientific notation is supported for both source code input and option.
Alternatively, the check for the floating point numbers can be disabled for
all floating point values by enabling the
\fBIgnoreAllFloatingPointValues\fP option.
.sp
Since values \fI0\fP and \fI0.0\fP are so common as the base counter of loops,
or initialization values for sums, they are always accepted without warning,
even if not present in the respective ignored values list.
.SS Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B IgnoredIntegerValues
Semicolon\-separated list of magic positive integers that will be accepted
without a warning. Default values are \fI{1, 2, 3, 4}\fP, and \fI0\fP is accepted
unconditionally.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B IgnorePowersOf2IntegerValues
Boolean value indicating whether to accept all powers\-of\-two integer values
without warning. Default value is \fIfalse\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B IgnoredFloatingPointValues
Semicolon\-separated list of magic positive floating point values that will
be accepted without a warning. Default values are \fI{1.0, 100.0}\fP and \fI0.0\fP
is accepted unconditionally.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B IgnoreAllFloatingPointValues
Boolean value indicating whether to accept all floating point values without
warning. Default value is \fIfalse\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B IgnoreBitFieldsWidths
Boolean value indicating whether to accept magic numbers as bit field widths
without warning. This is useful for example for register definitions which
are generated from hardware specifications. Default value is \fItrue\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B IgnoreTypeAliases
Boolean value indicating whether to accept magic numbers in \fBtypedef\fP or
\fBusing\fP declarations. Default value is \fIfalse\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B IgnoreUserDefinedLiterals
Boolean value indicating whether to accept magic numbers in user\-defined
literals. Default value is \fIfalse\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-make\-member\-function\-const
.sp
Finds non\-static member functions that can be made \fBconst\fP
because the functions don\(aqt use \fBthis\fP in a non\-const way.
.sp
This check tries to annotate methods according to
logical constness \%<https://\:isocpp\:.org/\:wiki/\:faq/\:const-correctness#\:logical-vs-physical-state>
(not physical constness).
Therefore, it will suggest to add a \fBconst\fP qualifier to a non\-const
method only if this method does something that is already possible though the
public interface on a \fBconst\fP pointer to the object:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
reading a public member variable
.IP \(bu 2
calling a public const\-qualified member function
.IP \(bu 2
returning const\-qualified \fBthis\fP
.IP \(bu 2
passing const\-qualified \fBthis\fP as a parameter.
.UNINDENT
.sp
This check will also suggest to add a \fBconst\fP qualifier to a non\-const
method if this method uses private data and functions in a limited number of
ways where logical constness and physical constness coincide:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
reading a member variable of builtin type
.UNINDENT
.sp
Specifically, this check will not suggest to add a \fBconst\fP to a non\-const
method if the method reads a private member variable of pointer type because
that allows to modify the pointee which might not preserve logical constness.
For the same reason, it does not allow to call private member functions
or member functions on private member variables.
.sp
In addition, this check ignores functions that
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
are declared \fBvirtual\fP
.IP \(bu 2
contain a \fBconst_cast\fP
.IP \(bu 2
are templated or part of a class template
.IP \(bu 2
have an empty body
.IP \(bu 2
do not (implicitly) use \fBthis\fP at all
(see readability\-convert\-member\-functions\-to\-static \%<>).
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following real\-world examples will be preserved by the check:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
class E1 {
  Pimpl &getPimpl() const;
public:
  int &get() {
    // Calling a private member function disables this check.
    return getPimpl()\->i;
  }
  ...
};

class E2 {
public:
  const int *get() const;
  // const_cast disables this check.
  S *get() {
    return const_cast<int*>(const_cast<const C*>(this)\->get());
  }
  ...
};
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
After applying modifications as suggested by the check, running the check again
might find more opportunities to mark member functions \fBconst\fP\&.
.SS readability\-math\-missing\-parentheses
.sp
Check for missing parentheses in mathematical expressions that involve
operators of different priorities.
.sp
Parentheses in mathematical expressions clarify the order
of operations, especially with different\-priority operators. Lengthy or
multiline expressions can obscure this order, leading to coding errors.
IDEs can aid clarity by highlighting parentheses. Explicitly using parentheses
also clarifies what the developer had in mind when writing the expression.
Ensuring their presence reduces ambiguity and errors, promoting clearer and
more maintainable code.
.sp
Before:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
int x = 1 + 2 * 3 \- 4 / 5;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
After:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
int x = 1 + (2 * 3) \- (4 / 5);
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-misleading\-indentation
.sp
Correct indentation helps to understand code. Mismatch of the syntactical
structure and the indentation of the code may hide serious problems.
Missing braces can also make it significantly harder to read the code,
therefore it is important to use braces.
.sp
The way to avoid dangling else is to always check that an \fBelse\fP belongs
to the \fBif\fP that begins in the same column.
.sp
You can omit braces when your inner part of e.g. an \fBif\fP statement has only
one statement in it. Although in that case you should begin the next statement
in the same column with the \fBif\fP\&.
.sp
Examples:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
// Dangling else:
if (cond1)
  if (cond2)
    foo1();
else
  foo2();  // Wrong indentation: else belongs to if(cond2) statement.

// Missing braces:
if (cond1)
  foo1();
  foo2();  // Not guarded by if(cond1).
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Limitations
.sp
Note that this check only works as expected when the tabs or spaces are used
consistently and not mixed.
.SS readability\-misplaced\-array\-index
.sp
This check warns for unusual array index syntax.
.sp
The following code has unusual array index syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
void f(int *X, int Y) {
  Y[X] = 0;
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
becomes
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
void f(int *X, int Y) {
  X[Y] = 0;
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B The check warns about such unusual syntax for readability reasons:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
There are programmers that are not familiar with this unusual syntax.
.IP \(bu 2
It is possible that variables are mixed up.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-named\-parameter
.sp
Find functions with unnamed arguments.
.sp
The check implements the following rule originating in the Google C++ Style
Guide:
.sp
\%<https://\:google\:.github\:.io/\:styleguide/\:cppguide\:.html#\:Function_Declarations_and_Definitions>
.sp
All parameters should have the same name in both the function declaration and
definition. If a parameter is not utilized, its name can be commented out in a
function definition.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
int doingSomething(int a, int b, int c);

int doingSomething(int a, int b, int /*c*/) {
    // Ok: the third param is not used
    return a + b;
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Corresponding cpplint.py check name: \fIreadability/function\fP\&.
.SS Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B InsertPlainNamesInForwardDecls
If set to \fItrue\fP, the check will insert parameter names without comments for
forward declarations only. Otherwise, the check will insert parameter names
as comments (e.g., \fB/*param*/\fP). Default is \fIfalse\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-non\-const\-parameter
.sp
The check finds function parameters of a pointer type that could be changed to
point to a constant type instead.
.sp
When \fBconst\fP is used properly, many mistakes can be avoided. Advantages when
using \fBconst\fP properly:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
prevent unintentional modification of data;
.IP \(bu 2
get additional warnings such as using uninitialized data;
.IP \(bu 2
make it easier for developers to see possible side effects.
.UNINDENT
.sp
This check is not strict about constness, it only warns when the constness will
make the function interface safer.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
// warning here; the declaration \(dqconst char *p\(dq would make the function
// interface safer.
char f1(char *p) {
  return *p;
}

// no warning; the declaration could be more const \(dqconst int * const p\(dq but
// that does not make the function interface safer.
int f2(const int *p) {
  return *p;
}

// no warning; making x const does not make the function interface safer
int f3(int x) {
  return x;
}

// no warning; Technically, *p can be const (\(dqconst struct S *p\(dq). But making
// *p const could be misleading. People might think that it\(aqs safe to pass
// const data to this function.
struct S { int *a; int *b; };
int f3(struct S *p) {
  *(p\->a) = 0;
}

// no warning; p is referenced by an lvalue.
void f4(int *p) {
  int &x = *p;
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-operators\-representation
.sp
Enforces consistent token representation for invoked binary, unary and
overloaded operators in C++ code. The check supports both traditional and
alternative representations of operators, such as \fB&&\fP and \fBand\fP, \fB||\fP
and \fBor\fP, and so on.
.sp
In the realm of C++ programming, developers have the option to choose between
two distinct representations for operators: traditional token representation
and alternative token representation. Traditional tokens utilize symbols,
such as \fB&&\fP, \fB||\fP, and \fB!\fP, while alternative tokens employ more
descriptive words like \fBand\fP, \fBor\fP, and \fBnot\fP\&.
.sp
In the following mapping table, a comprehensive list of traditional and
alternative tokens, along with their corresponding representations,
is presented:
.SS Token Representation Mapping Table
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Traditional
T}	T{
Alternative
T}
_
T{
\fB&&\fP
T}	T{
\fBand\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB&=\fP
T}	T{
\fBand_eq\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB&\fP
T}	T{
\fBbitand\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB|\fP
T}	T{
\fBbitor\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB~\fP
T}	T{
\fBcompl\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB!\fP
T}	T{
\fBnot\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB!=\fP
T}	T{
\fBnot_eq\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB||\fP
T}	T{
\fBor\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB|=\fP
T}	T{
\fBor_eq\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB^\fP
T}	T{
\fBxor\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB^=\fP
T}	T{
\fBxor_eq\fP
T}
.TE
.SS Example
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
// Traditional Token Representation:

if (!a||!b)
{
    // do something
}

// Alternative Token Representation:

if (not a or not b)
{
    // do something
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Options
.sp
Due to the distinct benefits and drawbacks of each representation, the default
configuration doesn\(aqt enforce either. Explicit configuration is needed.
.sp
To configure check to enforce Traditional Token Representation for all
operators set options to \fI&&;&=;&;|;~;!;!=;||;|=;^;^=\fP\&.
.sp
To configure check to enforce Alternative Token Representation for all
operators set options to
\fIand;and_eq;bitand;bitor;compl;not;not_eq;or;or_eq;xor;xor_eq\fP\&.
.sp
Developers do not need to enforce all operators, and can mix the
representations as desired by specifying a semicolon\-separated list of
both traditional and alternative tokens in the configuration,
such as \fIand;||;not\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B BinaryOperators
This option allows you to specify a semicolon\-separated list of binary
operators for which you want to enforce specific token representation.
The default value is empty string.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B OverloadedOperators
This option allows you to specify a semicolon\-separated list of overloaded
operators for which you want to enforce specific token representation.
The default value is empty string.
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-qualified\-auto
.sp
Adds pointer qualifications to \fBauto\fP\-typed variables that are deduced to
pointers.
.sp
LLVM Coding Standards \%<https://\:llvm\:.org/\:docs/\:CodingStandards\:.html#\:beware-unnecessary-copies-with-auto>
advises to make it obvious if a \fBauto\fP typed variable is a pointer. This
check will transform \fBauto\fP to \fBauto *\fP when the type is deduced to be a
pointer.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
for (auto Data : MutatablePtrContainer) {
  change(*Data);
}
for (auto Data : ConstantPtrContainer) {
  observe(*Data);
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Would be transformed into:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
for (auto *Data : MutatablePtrContainer) {
  change(*Data);
}
for (const auto *Data : ConstantPtrContainer) {
  observe(*Data);
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note \fBconst\fP \fBvolatile\fP qualified types will retain their \fBconst\fP and
\fBvolatile\fP qualifiers. Pointers to pointers will not be fully qualified.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
const auto Foo = cast<int *>(Baz1);
const auto Bar = cast<const int *>(Baz2);
volatile auto FooBar = cast<int *>(Baz3);
auto BarFoo = cast<int **>(Baz4);
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Would be transformed into:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
auto *const Foo = cast<int *>(Baz1);
const auto *const Bar = cast<const int *>(Baz2);
auto *volatile FooBar = cast<int *>(Baz3);
auto *BarFoo = cast<int **>(Baz4);
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B AddConstToQualified
When set to \fItrue\fP the check will add const qualifiers variables defined as
\fBauto *\fP or \fBauto &\fP when applicable.
Default value is \fItrue\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
auto Foo1 = cast<const int *>(Bar1);
auto *Foo2 = cast<const int *>(Bar2);
auto &Foo3 = cast<const int &>(Bar3);
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If AddConstToQualified is set to \fIfalse\fP, it will be transformed into:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
const auto *Foo1 = cast<const int *>(Bar1);
auto *Foo2 = cast<const int *>(Bar2);
auto &Foo3 = cast<const int &>(Bar3);
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Otherwise it will be transformed into:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
const auto *Foo1 = cast<const int *>(Bar1);
const auto *Foo2 = cast<const int *>(Bar2);
const auto &Foo3 = cast<const int &>(Bar3);
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note in the LLVM alias, the default value is \fIfalse\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B AllowedTypes
A semicolon\-separated list of names of types to ignore when \fBauto\fP is
deduced to that type or a pointer to that type. Note that this distinguishes
type aliases from the original type, so specifying e.g. \fBmy_int\fP will not
suppress reports about \fBint\fP even if it is defined as a \fBtypedef\fP alias
for \fBint\fP\&. Regular expressions are accepted, e.g. \fB[Rr]ef(erence)?$\fP
matches every type with suffix \fBRef\fP, \fBref\fP, \fBReference\fP and
\fBreference\fP\&. If a name in the list contains the sequence \fI::\fP it is matched
against the qualified type name (i.e. \fBnamespace::Type\fP), otherwise it is
matched against only the type name (i.e. \fBType\fP). E.g. to suppress reports
for \fBstd::array\fP iterators use \fIstd::array<.*>::(const_)?iterator\fP string.
The default is an empty string.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B IgnoreAliasing
If set to \fItrue\fP the check will use the underlying type to determine the type
that \fBauto\fP is deduced to. If set to \fIfalse\fP the check will not look beyond
the first type alias.
Default value is \fItrue\fP\&.
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
using IntPtr = int*;
IntPtr foo();

auto bar = foo();
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If \fBIgnoreAliasing\fP is set to \fItrue\fP, it will be transformed into:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
auto *bar = foo();
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Otherwise no changes will occur.
.UNINDENT
.SS Limitations
.sp
When \fBIgnoreAliasing\fP is set to \fIfalse\fP, there are cases where
Clang has not preserved the type alias and the underlying type will be used so
false positives may occur.
.sp
For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
using IntPtr = int *;

void loopPtr(const std::vector<IntPtr> &VectorIntPtr) {

  // May fail for IgnoreAliasing==false as AST does not have the \(aqIntPtr\(aq
  for (auto Data : VectorIntPtr) {
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-redundant\-access\-specifiers
.sp
Finds classes, structs, and unions containing redundant member (field and
method) access specifiers.
.SS Example
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
class Foo {
public:
  int x;
  int y;
public:
  int z;
protected:
  int a;
public:
  int c;
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the example above, the second \fBpublic\fP declaration can be removed without
any changes of behavior.
.SS Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B CheckFirstDeclaration
If set to \fItrue\fP, the check will also diagnose if the first access
specifier declaration is redundant (e.g. \fBprivate\fP inside \fBclass\fP,
or \fBpublic\fP inside \fBstruct\fP or \fBunion\fP).
Default is \fIfalse\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.SS Example
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
struct Bar {
public:
  int x;
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If \fICheckFirstDeclaration\fP option is enabled, a warning about redundant
access specifier will be emitted, because \fBpublic\fP is the default member access
for structs.
.SS readability\-redundant\-casting
.sp
Detects explicit type casting operations that involve the same source and
destination types, and subsequently recommend their removal. Covers a range of
explicit casting operations, including \fBstatic_cast\fP, \fBconst_cast\fP, C\-style
casts, and \fBreinterpret_cast\fP\&. Its primary objective is to enhance code
readability and maintainability by eliminating unnecessary type casting.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
int value = 42;
int result = static_cast<int>(value);
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In this example, the \fBstatic_cast<int>(value)\fP is redundant, as it performs
a cast from an \fBint\fP to another \fBint\fP\&.
.sp
Casting operations involving constructor conversions, user\-defined conversions,
functional casts, type\-dependent casts, casts between distinct type aliases
that refer to the same underlying type, as well as bitfield\-related casts and
casts directly from lvalue to rvalue, are all disregarded by the check.
.SS Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B IgnoreMacros
If set to \fItrue\fP, the check will not give warnings inside macros. Default
is \fItrue\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B IgnoreTypeAliases
When set to \fIfalse\fP, the check will consider type aliases, and when set to
\fItrue\fP, it will resolve all type aliases and operate on the underlying
types. Default is \fIfalse\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-redundant\-control\-flow
.sp
This check looks for procedures (functions returning no value) with \fBreturn\fP
statements at the end of the function. Such \fBreturn\fP statements are
redundant.
.sp
Loop statements (\fBfor\fP, \fBwhile\fP, \fBdo while\fP) are checked for redundant
\fBcontinue\fP statements at the end of the loop body.
.sp
Examples:
.sp
The following function \fIf\fP contains a redundant \fBreturn\fP statement:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
extern void g();
void f() {
  g();
  return;
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
becomes
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
extern void g();
void f() {
  g();
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following function \fIk\fP contains a redundant \fBcontinue\fP statement:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
void k() {
  for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {
    continue;
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
becomes
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
void k() {
  for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-redundant\-declaration
.sp
Finds redundant variable and function declarations.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
extern int X;
extern int X;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
becomes
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
extern int X;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Such redundant declarations can be removed without changing program behavior.
They can for instance be unintentional left overs from previous refactorings
when code has been moved around. Having redundant declarations could in worst
case mean that there are typos in the code that cause bugs.
.sp
Normally the code can be automatically fixed, \fBclang\-tidy\fP can remove
the second declaration. However there are 2 cases when you need to fix the code
manually:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
When the declarations are in different header files;
.IP \(bu 2
When multiple variables are declared together.
.UNINDENT
.SS Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B IgnoreMacros
If set to \fItrue\fP, the check will not give warnings inside macros. Default
is \fItrue\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-redundant\-function\-ptr\-dereference
.sp
Finds redundant dereferences of a function pointer.
.sp
Before:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
int f(int,int);
int (*p)(int, int) = &f;

int i = (**p)(10, 50);
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
After:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
int f(int,int);
int (*p)(int, int) = &f;

int i = (*p)(10, 50);
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-redundant\-inline\-specifier
.sp
Detects redundant \fBinline\fP specifiers on function and variable declarations.
.sp
Examples:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
constexpr inline void f() {}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the example above the keyword \fBinline\fP is redundant since constexpr
functions are implicitly inlined
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
class MyClass {
    inline void myMethod() {}
};
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the example above the keyword \fBinline\fP is redundant since member functions
defined entirely inside a class/struct/union definition are implicitly inlined.
.SS Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B StrictMode
If set to \fItrue\fP, the check will also flag functions and variables that
already have internal linkage as redundant. Default is \fIfalse\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-redundant\-member\-init
.sp
Finds member initializations that are unnecessary because the same default
constructor would be called if they were not present.
.SS Example
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
// Explicitly initializing the member s and v is unnecessary.
class Foo {
public:
  Foo() : s() {}

private:
  std::string s;
  std::vector<int> v {};
};
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B IgnoreBaseInCopyConstructors
Default is \fIfalse\fP\&.
.sp
When \fItrue\fP, the check will ignore unnecessary base class initializations
within copy constructors, since some compilers issue warnings/errors when
base classes are not explicitly initialized in copy constructors. For example,
\fBgcc\fP with \fB\-Wextra\fP or \fB\-Werror=extra\fP issues warning or error
\fBbase class \(aqBar\(aq should be explicitly initialized in the copy constructor\fP
if \fBBar()\fP were removed in the following example:
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
// Explicitly initializing member s and base class Bar is unnecessary.
struct Foo : public Bar {
  // Remove s() below. If IgnoreBaseInCopyConstructors!=0, keep Bar().
  Foo(const Foo& foo) : Bar(), s() {}
  std::string s;
};
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-redundant\-parentheses
.sp
Detect redundant parentheses.
.sp
When modifying code, one often forgets to remove the corresponding parentheses.
This results in overly lengthy code. When the expression is complex, finding
the matching parentheses becomes particularly difficult.
.SS Example
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
(1);
((a + 2)) * 3;
(a);
(\(dqaaa\(dq);
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Currently this check does not take into account the precedence of operations.
Even if the expression within the parentheses has a higher priority than that
outside the parentheses. In other words, removing the parentheses will not
affect the semantics.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
int a = (1 * 2) + 3; // no warning
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B AllowedDecls
Semicolon\-separated list of regular expressions matching names of declarations
to ignore when the parentheses are around. Declarations can include variables
or functions. The default is an \fIstd::max;std::min\fP\&.
.sp
Some STL library functions may have the same name as widely used function\-like
macro. For example, \fBstd::max\fP and \fBmax\fP macro. A workaround to distinguish
them is adding parentheses around functions to prevent function\-like macro.
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-redundant\-preprocessor
.sp
Finds potentially redundant preprocessor directives. At the moment the
following cases are detected:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fI#ifdef\fP .. \fI#endif\fP pairs which are nested inside an outer pair with the
same condition. For example:
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#ifdef FOO
#ifdef FOO // inner ifdef is considered redundant
void f();
#endif
#endif
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Same for \fI#ifndef\fP .. \fI#endif\fP pairs. For example:
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#ifndef FOO
#ifndef FOO // inner ifndef is considered redundant
void f();
#endif
#endif
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fI#ifndef\fP inside an \fI#ifdef\fP with the same condition:
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#ifdef FOO
#ifndef FOO // inner ifndef is considered redundant
void f();
#endif
#endif
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fI#ifdef\fP inside an \fI#ifndef\fP with the same condition:
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#ifndef FOO
#ifdef FOO // inner ifdef is considered redundant
void f();
#endif
#endif
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fI#if\fP .. \fI#endif\fP pairs which are nested inside an outer pair with the same
condition. For example:
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#define FOO 4
#if FOO == 4
#if FOO == 4 // inner if is considered redundant
void f();
#endif
#endif
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-redundant\-smartptr\-get
.sp
Find and remove redundant calls to smart pointer\(aqs \fB\&.get()\fP method.
.sp
Examples:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
ptr.get()\->Foo()  ==>  ptr\->Foo()
*ptr.get()  ==>  *ptr
*ptr\->get()  ==>  **ptr
if (ptr.get() == nullptr) ... => if (ptr == nullptr) ...
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B IgnoreMacros
If this option is set to \fItrue\fP (default is \fItrue\fP), the check will not warn
about calls inside macros.
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-redundant\-string\-cstr
.sp
Finds unnecessary calls to \fBstd::string::c_str()\fP and
\fBstd::string::data()\fP\&.
.SS Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B StringParameterFunctions
A semicolon\-separated list of regular expressions matching the
(fully qualified) names of function/method/operator, with the requirement
that any parameter currently accepting a \fBconst char*\fP input should also
be able to accept \fBstd::string\fP inputs, or proper overload candidates that
can do so should exist. This can be used to configure functions such as
\fBfmt::format\fP, \fBspdlog::logger::info\fP, or wrappers around these and
similar functions. The default value is the empty string.
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-redundant\-string\-init
.sp
Finds unnecessary string initializations.
.SS Examples
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
// Initializing string with empty string literal is unnecessary.
std::string a = \(dq\(dq;
std::string b(\(dq\(dq);

// becomes

std::string a;
std::string b;

// Initializing a string_view with an empty string literal produces an
// instance that compares equal to string_view().
std::string_view a = \(dq\(dq;
std::string_view b(\(dq\(dq);

// becomes
std::string_view a;
std::string_view b;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B StringNames
Default is \fI::std::basic_string;::std::basic_string_view\fP\&.
.sp
Semicolon\-delimited list of class names to apply this check to.
By default \fI::std::basic_string\fP applies to \fBstd::string\fP and
\fBstd::wstring\fP\&. Set to e.g. \fI::std::basic_string;llvm::StringRef;QString\fP
to perform this check on custom classes.
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-redundant\-typename
.sp
Finds redundant uses of the \fBtypename\fP keyword.
.sp
\fBtypename\fP is redundant in two cases. First, before non\-dependent names:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
/*typename*/ std::vector<int>::size_type size;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
And second, since C++20, before dependent names that appear in a context
where only a type is allowed (the following example shows just a few of them):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
template <typename T>
using trait = /*typename*/ T::type;

template <typename T>
/*typename*/ T::underlying_type as_underlying(T n) {
  return static_cast</*typename*/ T::underlying_type>(n);
}

template <typename T>
struct S {
  /*typename*/ T::type variable;
  /*typename*/ T::type function(/*typename*/ T::type);
};
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-reference\-to\-constructed\-temporary
.sp
Detects C++ code where a reference variable is used to extend the lifetime of
a temporary object that has just been constructed.
.sp
This construction is often the result of multiple code refactorings or a lack
of developer knowledge, leading to confusion or subtle bugs. In most cases,
what the developer really wanted to do is create a new variable rather than
extending the lifetime of a temporary object.
.sp
Examples of problematic code include:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
const std::string& str(\(dqhello\(dq);

struct Point { int x; int y; };
const Point& p = { 1, 2 };
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the first example, a \fBconst std::string&\fP reference variable \fBstr\fP is
assigned a temporary object created by the \fBstd::string(\(dqhello\(dq)\fP
constructor. In the second example, a \fBconst Point&\fP reference variable \fBp\fP
is assigned an object that is constructed from an initializer list
\fB{ 1, 2 }\fP\&. Both of these examples extend the lifetime of the temporary
object to the lifetime of the reference variable, which can make it difficult
to reason about and may lead to subtle bugs or misunderstanding.
.sp
To avoid these issues, it is recommended to change the reference variable to a
(\fBconst\fP) value variable.
.SS readability\-simplify\-boolean\-expr
.sp
Looks for boolean expressions involving boolean constants and simplifies
them to use the appropriate boolean expression directly.  Simplifies
boolean expressions by application of DeMorgan\(aqs Theorem.
.sp
Examples:
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Initial expression
T}	T{
Result
T}
_
T{
\fBif (b == true)\fP
T}	T{
\fBif (b)\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBif (b == false)\fP
T}	T{
\fBif (!b)\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBif (b && true)\fP
T}	T{
\fBif (b)\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBif (b && false)\fP
T}	T{
\fBif (false)\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBif (b || true)\fP
T}	T{
\fBif (true)\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBif (b || false)\fP
T}	T{
\fBif (b)\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBe ? true : false\fP
T}	T{
\fBe\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBe ? false : true\fP
T}	T{
\fB!e\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBif (true) t(); else f();\fP
T}	T{
\fBt();\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBif (false) t(); else f();\fP
T}	T{
\fBf();\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBif (e) return true; else return false;\fP
T}	T{
\fBreturn e;\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBif (e) return false; else return true;\fP
T}	T{
\fBreturn !e;\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBif (e) b = true; else b = false;\fP
T}	T{
\fBb = e;\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBif (e) b = false; else b = true;\fP
T}	T{
\fBb = !e;\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBif (e) return true; return false;\fP
T}	T{
\fBreturn e;\fP
T}
_
T{
\fBif (e) return false; return true;\fP
T}	T{
\fBreturn !e;\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB!(!a || b)\fP
T}	T{
\fBa && !b\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB!(a || !b)\fP
T}	T{
\fB!a && b\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB!(!a || !b)\fP
T}	T{
\fBa && b\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB!(!a && b)\fP
T}	T{
\fBa || !b\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB!(a && !b)\fP
T}	T{
\fB!a || b\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB!(!a && !b)\fP
T}	T{
\fBa || b\fP
T}
.TE
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B The resulting expression \fBe\fP is modified as follows:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP 1. 3
Unnecessary parentheses around the expression are removed.
.IP 2. 3
Negated applications of \fB!\fP are eliminated.
.IP 3. 3
Negated applications of comparison operators are changed to use the
opposite condition.
.IP 4. 3
Implicit conversions of pointers, including pointers to members, to
\fBbool\fP are replaced with explicit comparisons to \fBnullptr\fP in C++11
or \fBNULL\fP in C++98/03.
.IP 5. 3
Implicit casts to \fBbool\fP are replaced with explicit casts to \fBbool\fP\&.
.IP 6. 3
Object expressions with \fBexplicit operator bool\fP conversion operators
are replaced with explicit casts to \fBbool\fP\&.
.IP 7. 3
Implicit conversions of integral types to \fBbool\fP are replaced with
explicit comparisons to \fB0\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B Examples:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP 1. 3
The ternary assignment \fBbool b = (i < 0) ? true : false;\fP has redundant
parentheses and becomes \fBbool b = i < 0;\fP\&.
.IP 2. 3
The conditional return \fBif (!b) return false; return true;\fP has an
implied double negation and becomes \fBreturn b;\fP\&.
.IP 3. 3
The conditional return \fBif (i < 0) return false; return true;\fP becomes
\fBreturn i >= 0;\fP\&.
.sp
The conditional return \fBif (i != 0) return false; return true;\fP becomes
\fBreturn i == 0;\fP\&.
.IP 4. 3
The conditional return \fBif (p) return true; return false;\fP has an
implicit conversion of a pointer to \fBbool\fP and becomes
\fBreturn p != nullptr;\fP\&.
.sp
The ternary assignment \fBbool b = (i & 1) ? true : false;\fP has an
implicit conversion of \fBi & 1\fP to \fBbool\fP and becomes
\fBbool b = (i & 1) != 0;\fP\&.
.IP 5. 3
The conditional return \fBif (i & 1) return true; else return false;\fP has
an implicit conversion of an integer quantity \fBi & 1\fP to \fBbool\fP and
becomes \fBreturn (i & 1) != 0;\fP
.IP 6. 3
Given \fBstruct X { explicit operator bool(); };\fP, and an instance \fBx\fP
of \fBstruct X\fP, the conditional return
\fBif (x) return true; return false;\fP
becomes \fBreturn static_cast<bool>(x);\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B IgnoreMacros
If \fItrue\fP, ignore boolean expressions originating from expanded macros.
Default is \fIfalse\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B ChainedConditionalReturn
If \fItrue\fP, conditional boolean return statements at the end of an
\fBif/else if\fP chain will be transformed. Default is \fIfalse\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B ChainedConditionalAssignment
If \fItrue\fP, conditional boolean assignments at the end of an \fBif/else
if\fP chain will be transformed. Default is \fIfalse\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B SimplifyDeMorgan
If \fItrue\fP, DeMorgan\(aqs Theorem will be applied to simplify negated
conjunctions and disjunctions.  Default is \fItrue\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B SimplifyDeMorganRelaxed
If \fItrue\fP, \fBSimplifyDeMorgan\fP will also transform negated
conjunctions and disjunctions where there is no negation on either operand.
This option has no effect if \fBSimplifyDeMorgan\fP is \fIfalse\fP\&.
Default is \fIfalse\fP\&.
.sp
When Enabled:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
bool X = !(A && B)
bool Y = !(A || B)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Would be transformed to:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
bool X = !A || !B
bool Y = !A && !B
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-simplify\-subscript\-expr
.sp
This check simplifies subscript expressions. Currently this covers calling
\fB\&.data()\fP and immediately doing an array subscript operation to obtain a
single element, in which case simply calling \fBoperator[]\fP suffice.
.sp
Examples:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
std::string s = ...;
char c = s.data()[i];  // char c = s[i];
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B Types
The list of type(s) that triggers this check. Default is
\fI::std::basic_string;::std::basic_string_view;::std::vector;::std::array;::std::span\fP
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-static\-accessed\-through\-instance
.sp
Checks for member expressions that access static members through instances, and
replaces them with uses of the appropriate qualified\-id.
.sp
Example:
.sp
The following code:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
struct C {
  static void foo();
  static int x;
  enum { E1 };
  enum E { E2 };
};

C *c1 = new C();
c1\->foo();
c1\->x;
c1\->E1;
c1\->E2;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
is changed to:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
C *c1 = new C();
C::foo();
C::x;
C::E1;
C::E2;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fI\-\-fix\fP commandline option provides default support for safe fixes, whereas
\fI\-\-fix\-notes\fP enables fixes that may replace expressions with side effects,
potentially altering the program\(aqs behavior.
.SS readability\-static\-definition\-in\-anonymous\-namespace
.sp
Finds static function and variable definitions in anonymous namespace.
.sp
In this case, \fBstatic\fP is redundant, because anonymous namespace limits the
visibility of definitions to a single translation unit.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
namespace {
  static int a = 1; // Warning.
  static const int b = 1; // Warning.
  namespace inner {
    static int c = 1; // Warning.
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The check will apply a fix by removing the redundant \fBstatic\fP qualifier.
.SS readability\-string\-compare
.sp
Finds string comparisons using the compare method.
.sp
A common mistake is to use the string\(aqs \fBcompare\fP method instead of using the
equality or inequality operators. The compare method is intended for sorting
functions and thus returns a negative number, a positive number or
zero depending on the lexicographical relationship between the strings
compared. If an equality or inequality check can suffice, that is recommended.
This is recommended to avoid the risk of incorrect interpretation of the return
value and to simplify the code. The string equality and inequality operators
can also be faster than the \fBcompare\fP method due to early termination.
.SS Example
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
// The same rules apply to std::string_view.
std::string str1{\(dqa\(dq};
std::string str2{\(dqb\(dq};

// use str1 != str2 instead.
if (str1.compare(str2)) {
}

// use str1 == str2 instead.
if (!str1.compare(str2)) {
}

// use str1 == str2 instead.
if (str1.compare(str2) == 0) {
}

// use str1 != str2 instead.
if (str1.compare(str2) != 0) {
}

// use str1 == str2 instead.
if (0 == str1.compare(str2)) {
}

// use str1 != str2 instead.
if (0 != str1.compare(str2)) {
}

// Use str1 == \(dqfoo\(dq instead.
if (str1.compare(\(dqfoo\(dq) == 0) {
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The above code examples show the list of if\-statements that this check will
give a warning for. All of them use \fBcompare\fP to check equality or
inequality of two strings instead of using the correct operators.
.SS Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B StringLikeClasses
A string containing semicolon\-separated names of string\-like classes.
By default contains only \fB::std::basic_string\fP
and \fB::std::basic_string_view\fP\&. If a class from this list has
a \fBcompare\fP method similar to that of \fBstd::string\fP, it will be checked
in the same way.
.UNINDENT
.SS Example
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
struct CustomString {
public:
  int compare (const CustomString& other) const;
}

CustomString str1;
CustomString str2;

// use str1 != str2 instead.
if (str1.compare(str2)) {
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If \fIStringLikeClasses\fP contains \fBCustomString\fP, the check will suggest
replacing \fBcompare\fP with equality operator.
.SS readability\-suspicious\-call\-argument
.sp
Finds function calls where the arguments passed are provided out of order,
based on the difference between the argument name and the parameter names
of the function.
.sp
Given a function call \fBf(foo, bar);\fP and a function signature
\fBvoid f(T tvar, U uvar)\fP, the arguments \fBfoo\fP and \fBbar\fP are swapped if
\fBfoo\fP (the argument name) is more similar to \fBuvar\fP (the other parameter)
than \fBtvar\fP (the parameter it is currently passed to) \fBand\fP \fBbar\fP is
more similar to \fBtvar\fP than \fBuvar\fP\&.
.sp
Warnings might indicate either that the arguments are swapped, or that the
names\(aq cross\-similarity might hinder code comprehension.
.SS Heuristics
.sp
The following heuristics are implemented in the check.
If \fBany\fP of the enabled heuristics deem the arguments to be provided out of
order, a warning will be issued.
.sp
The heuristics themselves are implemented by considering pairs of strings, and
are symmetric, so in the following there is no distinction on which string is
the argument name and which string is the parameter name.
.SS Equality
.sp
The most trivial heuristic, which compares the two strings for case\-insensitive
equality.
.SS Abbreviation
.sp
Common abbreviations can be specified which will deem the strings similar if
the abbreviated and the abbreviation stand together.
For example, if \fBsrc\fP is registered as an abbreviation for \fBsource\fP, then
the following code example will be warned about.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
void foo(int source, int x);

foo(b, src);
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The abbreviations to recognise can be configured with the
Abbreviations check option.
This heuristic is case\-insensitive.
.SS Prefix
.sp
The \fIprefix\fP heuristic reports if one of the strings is a sufficiently long
prefix of the other string, e.g. \fBtarget\fP to \fBtargetPtr\fP\&.
The similarity percentage is the length ratio of the prefix to the longer
string, in the previous example, it would be \fI6 / 9 = 66.66...\fP%.
.sp
This heuristic can be configured with bounds\&.
The default bounds are: below \fI25\fP% dissimilar and above \fI30\fP% similar.
This heuristic is case\-insensitive.
.SS Suffix
.sp
Analogous to the \fIPrefix\fP heuristic.
In the case of \fBoldValue\fP and \fBvalue\fP compared, the similarity percentage
is \fI8 / 5 = 62.5\fP%.
.sp
This heuristic can be configured with bounds\&.
The default bounds are: below \fI25\fP% dissimilar and above \fI30\fP% similar.
This heuristic is case\-insensitive.
.SS Substring
.sp
The substring heuristic combines the prefix and the suffix heuristic, and tries
to find the \fIlongest common substring\fP in the two strings provided.
The similarity percentage is the ratio of the found longest common substring
against the \fIlonger\fP of the two input strings.
For example, given \fBval\fP and \fBrvalue\fP, the similarity is \fI3 / 6 = 50\fP%.
If no characters are common in the two string, \fI0\fP%.
.sp
This heuristic can be configured with bounds\&.
The default bounds are: below \fI40\fP% dissimilar and above \fI50\fP% similar.
This heuristic is case\-insensitive.
.SS Levenshtein distance (as \fILevenshtein\fP)
.sp
The Levenshtein distance \%<http://\:en\:.wikipedia\:.org/\:wiki/\:Levenshtein_distance>
describes how many single\-character changes (additions, changes, or removals)
must be applied to transform one string into another.
.sp
The Levenshtein distance is translated into a similarity percentage by dividing
it with the length of the \fIlonger\fP string, and taking its complement with
regards to \fI100\fP%.
For example, given \fBsomething\fP and \fBanything\fP, the distance is \fI4\fP edits,
and the similarity percentage is \fI100\fP% \fI\- 4 / 9 = 55.55...\fP%.
.sp
This heuristic can be configured with bounds\&.
The default bounds are: below \fI50\fP% dissimilar and above \fI66\fP% similar.
This heuristic is case\-sensitive.
.SS Jaro\-\-Winkler distance (as \fIJaroWinkler\fP)
.sp
The Jaro\-\-Winkler distance \%<http://\:en\:.wikipedia\:.org/\:wiki/\:Jaro–Winkler_distance>
is an edit distance like the Levenshtein distance.
It is calculated from the amount of common characters that are sufficiently
close to each other in position, and to\-be\-changed characters.
The original definition of Jaro has been extended by Winkler to weigh prefix
similarities more.
The similarity percentage is expressed as an average of the common and
non\-common characters against the length of both strings.
.sp
This heuristic can be configured with bounds\&.
The default bounds are: below \fI75\fP% dissimilar and above \fI85\fP% similar.
This heuristic is case\-insensitive.
.SS Sørensen\-\-Dice coefficient (as \fIDice\fP)
.sp
The Sørensen\-\-Dice coefficient \%<http://\:en\:.wikipedia\:.org/\:wiki/\:Sørensen–Dice_coefficient>
was originally defined to measure the similarity of two sets.
Formally, the coefficient is calculated by dividing \fI2 * #(intersection)\fP with
\fI#(set1) + #(set2)\fP, where \fI#()\fP is the cardinality function of sets.
This metric is applied to strings by creating bigrams (substring sequences of
length 2) of the two strings and using the set of bigrams for the two strings
as the two sets.
.sp
This heuristic can be configured with bounds\&.
The default bounds are: below \fI60\fP% dissimilar and above \fI70\fP% similar.
This heuristic is case\-insensitive.
.SS Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B MinimumIdentifierNameLength
Sets the minimum required length the argument and parameter names
need to have. Names shorter than this length will be ignored.
Defaults to \fI3\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B Abbreviations
For the \fBAbbreviation\fP heuristic
(see here), this option configures the
abbreviations in the \fI\(dqabbreviation=abbreviated_value\(dq\fP format.
The option is a string, with each value joined by \fI\(dq;\(dq\fP\&.
.sp
By default, the following abbreviations are set:
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fIaddr=address\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIarr=array\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIattr=attribute\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIbuf=buffer\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIcl=client\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIcnt=count\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIcol=column\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIcpy=copy\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIdest=destination\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIdist=distance\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIdst=distance\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIelem=element\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIhght=height\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIi=index\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIidx=index\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIlen=length\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIln=line\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIlst=list\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fInr=number\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fInum=number\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIpos=position\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIptr=pointer\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIref=reference\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIsrc=source\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIsrv=server\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIstmt=statement\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIstr=string\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIval=value\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIvar=variable\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIvec=vector\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fIwdth=width\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The configuration options for each implemented heuristic (see above) is
constructed dynamically.
In the following, \fI<HeuristicName>\fP refers to one of the keys from the
heuristics implemented.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B <HeuristicName>
\fITrue\fP or \fIFalse\fP, whether a particular heuristic, such as \fIEquality\fP or
\fILevenshtein\fP is enabled.
.sp
Defaults to \fITrue\fP for every heuristic.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B <HeuristicName>DissimilarBelow, <HeuristicName>SimilarAbove
A value between \fI0\fP and \fI100\fP, expressing a percentage.
The bounds set what percentage of similarity the heuristic must deduce
for the two identifiers to be considered similar or dissimilar by the
check.
.sp
Given arguments \fBarg1\fP and \fBarg2\fP passed to \fBparam1\fP and \fBparam2\fP,
respectively, the bounds check is performed in the following way:
If the similarity of the currently passed argument order
(\fBarg1\fP to \fBparam1\fP) is \fBbelow\fP the \fIDissimilarBelow\fP threshold, and
the similarity of the suggested swapped order (\fBarg1\fP to \fBparam2\fP) is
\fBabove\fP the \fISimilarAbove\fP threshold, the swap is reported.
.sp
For the defaults of each heuristic, see above\&.
.UNINDENT
.SS Name synthesis
.sp
When comparing the argument names and parameter names, the following logic is
used to gather the names for comparison:
.sp
Parameter names are the identifiers as written in the source code.
.sp
Argument names are:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
If a variable is passed, the variable\(aqs name.
.IP \(bu 2
If a subsequent function call\(aqs return value is used as argument, the
called function\(aqs name.
.IP \(bu 2
Otherwise, empty string.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Empty argument or parameter names are ignored by the heuristics.
.SS readability\-uniqueptr\-delete\-release
.sp
Replace \fBdelete <unique_ptr>.release()\fP with \fB<unique_ptr> = nullptr\fP\&.
The latter is shorter, simpler and does not require use of raw pointer APIs.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
std::unique_ptr<int> P;
delete P.release();

// becomes

std::unique_ptr<int> P;
P = nullptr;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B PreferResetCall
If \fItrue\fP, refactor by calling the reset member function instead of
assigning to \fBnullptr\fP\&. Default value is \fIfalse\fP\&.
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
std::unique_ptr<int> P;
delete P.release();

// becomes

std::unique_ptr<int> P;
P.reset();
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-uppercase\-literal\-suffix
.sp
\fIcert\-dcl16\-c\fP redirects here as an alias for this check.
By default, only the suffixes that begin with \fBl\fP (\fBl\fP, \fBll\fP, \fBlu\fP,
\fBllu\fP, but not \fBu\fP, \fBul\fP, \fBull\fP) are diagnosed by that alias.
.sp
\fIhicpp\-uppercase\-literal\-suffix\fP redirects here as an alias for this check.
.sp
Detects when the integral literal or floating point (decimal or hexadecimal)
literal has a non\-uppercase suffix and provides a fix\-it hint with the
uppercase suffix.
.sp
All valid combinations of suffixes are supported.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
auto x = 1;  // OK, no suffix.

auto x = 1u; // warning: integer literal suffix \(aqu\(aq is not upper\-case

auto x = 1U; // OK, suffix is uppercase.

\&...
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B NewSuffixes
Optionally, a list of the destination suffixes can be provided. When the
suffix is found, a case\-insensitive lookup in that list is made, and if a
replacement is found that is different from the current suffix, then the
diagnostic is issued. This allows for fine\-grained control of what suffixes to
consider and what their replacements should be.
.UNINDENT
.SS Example
.sp
Given a list \fIL;uL\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBl\fP \-> \fBL\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBL\fP will be kept as is.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBul\fP \-> \fBuL\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBUl\fP \-> \fBuL\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBUL\fP \-> \fBuL\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBuL\fP will be kept as is.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBull\fP will be kept as is, since it is not in the list
.IP \(bu 2
and so on.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B IgnoreMacros
If this option is set to \fItrue\fP (default is \fItrue\fP), the check will not warn
about literal suffixes inside macros.
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-use\-anyofallof
.sp
Finds range\-based for loops that can be replaced by a call to
\fBstd::any_of\fP or \fBstd::all_of\fP\&. In C++20 mode, suggests
\fBstd::ranges::any_of\fP or \fBstd::ranges::all_of\fP\&.
.sp
Example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
bool all_even(std::vector<int> V) {
  for (int I : V) {
    if (I % 2)
      return false;
  }
  return true;
  // Replace loop by
  // return std::ranges::all_of(V, [](int I) { return I % 2 == 0; });
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-use\-concise\-preprocessor\-directives
.sp
Finds uses of \fB#if\fP that can be simplified to \fB#ifdef\fP or \fB#ifndef\fP and,
since C23 and C++23, uses of \fB#elif\fP that can be simplified to \fB#elifdef\fP
or \fB#elifndef\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#if defined(MEOW)
#if !defined(MEOW)

// becomes

#ifdef MEOW
#ifndef MEOW
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Since C23 and C++23:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#elif defined(MEOW)
#elif !defined(MEOW)

// becomes

#elifdef MEOW
#elifndef MEOW
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS readability\-use\-std\-min\-max
.sp
Replaces certain conditional statements with equivalent calls to
\fBstd::min\fP or \fBstd::max\fP\&.
Note: This may impact performance in critical code due to potential
additional stores compared to the original if statement.
.sp
Before:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
void foo() {
  int a = 2, b = 3;
  if (a < b)
    a = b;
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
After:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
void foo() {
  int a = 2, b = 3;
  a = std::max(a, b);
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS zircon\-temporary\-objects
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fIzircon\-temporary\-objects\fP check has been deprecated and will be removed
in 24th release of LLVM. Please use
fuchsia\-temporary\-objects \%<> instead.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.TS
box center;
l|l.
