Checks related to Zircon kernel coding conventions.
T}
.TE
.sp
Clang diagnostics are treated in a similar way as check diagnostics. Clang
diagnostics are displayed by \fBclang\-tidy\fP and can be filtered out
using the \fB\-checks=\fP option. However, the \fB\-checks=\fP option does not
affect compilation arguments, so it cannot turn on Clang warnings which are
not already turned on in the build configuration. The \fB\-warnings\-as\-errors=\fP
option upgrades any warnings emitted under the \fB\-checks=\fP flag to errors (but
it does not enable any checks itself).
.sp
Clang diagnostics have check names starting with \fBclang\-diagnostic\-\fP\&.
Diagnostics which have a corresponding warning option, are named
\fBclang\-diagnostic\-<warning\-option>\fP, e.g. Clang warning controlled by
\fB\-Wliteral\-conversion\fP will be reported with check name
\fBclang\-diagnostic\-literal\-conversion\fP\&.
.sp
Clang compiler errors (such as syntax errors, semantic errors, or other
failures that prevent Clang from compiling the code) are reported with the
check name \fBclang\-diagnostic\-error\fP\&. These represent fundamental compilation
failures that must be fixed before \fBclang\-tidy\fP can perform its
analysis. Unlike other diagnostics, \fBclang\-diagnostic\-error\fP cannot be
disabled, as \fBclang\-tidy\fP requires valid code to function.
.sp
The \fB\-fix\fP flag instructs \fBclang\-tidy\fP to fix found errors if
supported by corresponding checks.
.sp
An overview of all the command\-line options:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ clang\-tidy \-\-help
USAGE: clang\-tidy [options] <source0> [... <sourceN>]

OPTIONS:

Generic Options:

  \-\-help                           \- Display available options (\-\-help\-hidden for more)
  \-\-help\-list                      \- Display list of available options (\-\-help\-list\-hidden for more)
  \-\-version                        \- Display the version of this program

clang\-tidy options:

  \-\-checks=<string>                \- Comma\-separated list of globs with optional \(aq\-\(aq
                                     prefix. Globs are processed in order of
                                     appearance in the list. Globs without \(aq\-\(aq
                                     prefix add checks with matching names to the
                                     set, globs with the \(aq\-\(aq prefix remove checks
                                     with matching names from the set of enabled
                                     checks. This option\(aqs value is appended to the
                                     value of the \(aqChecks\(aq option in .clang\-tidy
                                     file, if any.
  \-\-config=<string>                \- Specifies a configuration in YAML/JSON format:
                                       \-config=\(dq{Checks: \(aq*\(aq,
                                                 CheckOptions: {x: y}}\(dq
                                     When the value is empty, clang\-tidy will
                                     attempt to find a file named .clang\-tidy for
                                     each source file in its parent directories.
  \-\-config\-file=<string>           \- Specify the path of .clang\-tidy or custom config file:
                                      e.g. \-\-config\-file=/some/path/myTidyConfigFile
                                     This option internally works exactly the same way as
                                      \-\-config option after reading specified config file.
                                     Use either \-\-config\-file or \-\-config, not both.
  \-\-dump\-config                    \- Dumps configuration in the YAML format to
                                     stdout. This option can be used along with a
                                     file name (and \(aq\-\-\(aq if the file is outside of a
                                     project with configured compilation database).
                                     The configuration used for this file will be
                                     printed.
                                     Use along with \-checks=* to include
                                     configuration of all checks.
  \-\-enable\-check\-profile           \- Enable per\-check timing profiles, and print a
                                     report to stderr.
  \-\-enable\-module\-headers\-parsing  \- Enables preprocessor\-level module header parsing
                                     for C++20 and above, empowering specific checks
                                     to detect macro definitions within modules. This
                                     feature may cause performance and parsing issues
                                     and is therefore considered experimental.
  \-\-exclude\-header\-filter=<string> \- Regular expression matching the names of the
                                     headers to exclude diagnostics from. Diagnostics
                                     from the main file of each translation unit are
                                     always displayed.
                                     Must be used together with \-\-header\-filter.
                                     Can be used together with \-line\-filter.
                                     This option overrides the \(aqExcludeHeaderFilterRegex\(aq
                                     option in .clang\-tidy file, if any.
  \-\-explain\-config                 \- For each enabled check explains, where it is
                                     enabled, i.e. in clang\-tidy binary, command
                                     line or a specific configuration file.
  \-\-export\-fixes=<filename>        \- YAML file to store suggested fixes in. The
                                     stored fixes can be applied to the input source
                                     code with clang\-apply\-replacements.
  \-\-extra\-arg=<string>             \- Additional argument to append to the compiler command line
  \-\-extra\-arg\-before=<string>      \- Additional argument to prepend to the compiler command line
  \-\-fix                            \- Apply suggested fixes. Without \-fix\-errors
                                     clang\-tidy will bail out if any compilation
                                     errors were found.
  \-\-fix\-errors                     \- Apply suggested fixes even if compilation
                                     errors were found. If compiler errors have
                                     attached fix\-its, clang\-tidy will apply them as
                                     well.
  \-\-fix\-notes                      \- If a warning has no fix, but a single fix can
                                     be found through an associated diagnostic note,
                                     apply the fix.
                                     Specifying this flag will implicitly enable the
                                     \(aq\-\-fix\(aq flag.
  \-\-format\-style=<string>          \- Style for formatting code around applied fixes:
                                       \- \(aqnone\(aq (default) turns off formatting
                                       \- \(aqfile\(aq (literally \(aqfile\(aq, not a placeholder)
                                         uses .clang\-format file in the closest parent
                                         directory
                                       \- \(aq{ <json> }\(aq specifies options inline, e.g.
                                         \-format\-style=\(aq{BasedOnStyle: llvm, IndentWidth: 8}\(aq
                                       \- \(aqllvm\(aq, \(aqgoogle\(aq, \(aqwebkit\(aq, \(aqmozilla\(aq
                                     See clang\-format documentation for the up\-to\-date
                                     information about formatting styles and options.
                                     This option overrides the \(aqFormatStyle\(ga option in
                                     .clang\-tidy file, if any.
  \-\-header\-filter=<string>         \- Regular expression matching the names of the
                                     headers to output diagnostics from. The default
                                     value is \(aq.*\(aq, i.e. diagnostics from all non\-system
                                     headers are displayed by default. Diagnostics
                                     from the main file of each translation unit are
                                     always displayed.
                                     Can be used together with \-line\-filter.
                                     This option overrides the \(aqHeaderFilterRegex\(aq
                                     option in .clang\-tidy file, if any.
  \-\-line\-filter=<string>           \- List of files and line ranges to output diagnostics from.
                                     The range is inclusive on both ends. Can be used together
                                     with \-header\-filter. The format of the list is a JSON
                                     array of objects. For example:

                                       [
                                         {\(dqname\(dq:\(dqfile1.cpp\(dq,\(dqlines\(dq:[[1,3],[5,7]]},
                                         {\(dqname\(dq:\(dqfile2.h\(dq}
                                       ]

                                     This will output diagnostics from \(aqfile1.cpp\(aq only for
                                     the line ranges [1,3] and [5,7], as well as all from the
                                     entire \(aqfile2.h\(aq.
  \-\-list\-checks                    \- List all enabled checks and exit. Use with
                                     \-checks=* to list all available checks.
  \-\-load=<pluginfilename>          \- Load the specified plugin
  \-p <string>                      \- Build path
  \-\-quiet                          \- Run clang\-tidy in quiet mode. This suppresses
                                     printing statistics about ignored warnings and
                                     warnings treated as errors if the respective
                                     options are specified.
  \-\-store\-check\-profile=<prefix>   \- By default reports are printed in tabulated
                                     format to stderr. When this option is passed,
                                     these per\-TU profiles are instead stored as JSON.
  \-\-system\-headers                 \- Display the errors from system headers.
                                     This option overrides the \(aqSystemHeaders\(aq option
                                     in .clang\-tidy file, if any.
  \-\-use\-color                      \- Use colors in diagnostics. If not set, colors
                                     will be used if the terminal connected to
                                     standard output supports colors.
                                     This option overrides the \(aqUseColor\(aq option in
                                     .clang\-tidy file, if any.
  \-\-verify\-config                  \- Check the config files to ensure each check and
                                     option is recognized.
  \-\-vfsoverlay=<filename>          \- Overlay the virtual filesystem described by file
                                     over the real file system.
  \-\-warnings\-as\-errors=<string>    \- Upgrades warnings to errors. Same format as
                                     \(aq\-checks\(aq.
                                     This option\(aqs value is appended to the value of
                                     the \(aqWarningsAsErrors\(aq option in .clang\-tidy
                                     file, if any.
  \-\-allow\-no\-checks                \- Allow empty enabled checks. This suppresses
                                     the \(dqno checks enabled\(dq error when disabling
                                     all of the checks.

\-p <build\-path> is used to read a compile command database.

  For example, it can be a CMake build directory in which a file named
  compile_commands.json exists (use \-DCMAKE_EXPORT_COMPILE_COMMANDS=ON
  CMake option to get this output). When no build path is specified,
  a search for compile_commands.json will be attempted through all
  parent paths of the first input file . See:
  https://clang.llvm.org/docs/HowToSetupToolingForLLVM.html for an
  example of setting up Clang Tooling on a source tree.

<source0> ... specify the paths of source files. These paths are
  looked up in the compile command database. If the path of a file is
  absolute, it needs to point into CMake\(aqs source tree. If the path is
  relative, the current working directory needs to be in the CMake
  source tree and the file must be in a subdirectory of the current
  working directory. \(dq./\(dq prefixes in the relative files will be
  automatically removed, but the rest of a relative path must be a
  suffix of a path in the compile command database.

Parameters files:
  A large number of options or source files can be passed as parameter files
  by use \(aq@parameter\-file\(aq in the command line.

Configuration files:
  clang\-tidy attempts to read configuration for each source file from a
  .clang\-tidy file located in the closest parent directory of the source
  file. The .clang\-tidy file is specified in YAML format. If any configuration
  options have a corresponding command\-line option, command\-line option takes
  precedence.

  The following configuration options may be used in a .clang\-tidy file:

  CheckOptions                 \- List of key\-value pairs defining check\-specific
                                 options. Example:
                                   CheckOptions:
                                     some\-check.SomeOption: \(aqsome value\(aq
  Checks                       \- Same as \(aq\-\-checks\(aq. Additionally, the list of
                                 globs can be specified as a list instead of a
                                 string.
  CustomChecks                 \- List of user defined checks based on
                                 Clang\-Query syntax.
  ExcludeHeaderFilterRegex     \- Same as \(aq\-\-exclude\-header\-filter\(aq.
  ExtraArgs                    \- Same as \(aq\-\-extra\-arg\(aq.
  ExtraArgsBefore              \- Same as \(aq\-\-extra\-arg\-before\(aq.
  FormatStyle                  \- Same as \(aq\-\-format\-style\(aq.
  HeaderFileExtensions         \- File extensions to consider to determine if a
                                 given diagnostic is located in a header file.
  HeaderFilterRegex            \- Same as \(aq\-\-header\-filter\(aq.
  ImplementationFileExtensions \- File extensions to consider to determine if a
                                 given diagnostic is located in an
                                 implementation file.
  InheritParentConfig          \- If this option is true in a config file, the
                                 configuration file in the parent directory
                                 (if any exists) will be taken and the current
                                 config file will be applied on top of the
                                 parent one.
  SystemHeaders                \- Same as \(aq\-\-system\-headers\(aq.
  UseColor                     \- Same as \(aq\-\-use\-color\(aq.
  User                         \- Specifies the name or e\-mail of the user
                                 running clang\-tidy. This option is used, for
                                 example, to place the correct user name in
                                 TODO() comments in the relevant check.
  WarningsAsErrors             \- Same as \(aq\-\-warnings\-as\-errors\(aq.
  RemovedArgs                  \- Same as \(aq\-\-removed\-arg\(aq

  The effective configuration can be inspected using \-\-dump\-config:

    $ clang\-tidy \-\-dump\-config
    \-\-\-
    Checks:              \(aq\-*,some\-check\(aq
    WarningsAsErrors:    \(aq\(aq
    HeaderFileExtensions:         [\(aq\(aq, \(aqh\(aq,\(aqhh\(aq,\(aqhpp\(aq,\(aqhxx\(aq]
    ImplementationFileExtensions: [\(aqc\(aq,\(aqcc\(aq,\(aqcpp\(aq,\(aqcxx\(aq]
    HeaderFilterRegex:   \(aq.*\(aq
    FormatStyle:         none
    InheritParentConfig: true
    User:                user
    CheckOptions:
      some\-check.SomeOption: \(aqsome value\(aq
    ...
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Clang\-Tidy Automation
.sp
\fBclang\-tidy\fP can analyze multiple source files by specifying them on
the command line. For larger projects, automation scripts provide additional
functionality like parallel execution and integration with version control
systems.
.SS Running Clang\-Tidy in Parallel
.sp
\fBclang\-tidy\fP can process multiple files sequentially, but for projects
with many source files, the \fBrun\-clang\-tidy.py\fP script provides
parallel execution to significantly reduce analysis time. This script is
included with clang\-tidy and runs \fBclang\-tidy\fP over all files in a
compilation database or a specified path concurrently.
.sp
The script requires a compilation database (\fBcompile_commands.json\fP) which
can be generated by build systems like CMake (using
\fB\-DCMAKE_EXPORT_COMPILE_COMMANDS=ON\fP) or by tools like Bear \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:rizsotto/\:Bear>\&.
.sp
The script supports most of the same options as \fBclang\-tidy\fP itself,
including \fB\-checks=\fP, \fB\-fix\fP, \fB\-header\-filter=\fP, and configuration
options. Run \fBrun\-clang\-tidy.py \-\-help\fP for a complete list of available
options.
.sp
Example invocations:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# Run clang\-tidy on all files in the compilation database in parallel
$ run\-clang\-tidy.py \-p=build/

# Run with specific checks and apply fixes
$ run\-clang\-tidy.py \-p=build/ \-fix \-checks=\-*,readability\-*

# Run on specific files/directories with header filtering
$ run\-clang\-tidy.py \-p=build/ \-header\-filter=src/ src/

# Run with parallel execution (uses all CPU cores by default)
$ run\-clang\-tidy.py \-p=build/ \-j 4
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Running Clang\-Tidy on Diff
.sp
The \fBclang\-tidy\-diff.py\fP script allows you to run
\fBclang\-tidy\fP on the lines that have been modified in your working
directory or in a specific diff. Importantly, \fBclang\-tidy\-diff.py\fP only reports
diagnostics for changed lines; \fBclang\-tidy\fP still analyzes the entire
file and filters out unchanged lines after analysis, so this does not improve
performance. This is particularly useful for code reviews and continuous
integration, as it focuses analysis on the changed code rather than the entire
codebase.
.sp
The script can work with various diff sources:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Git working directory changes
.IP \(bu 2
Output from \fBgit diff\fP
.IP \(bu 2
Output from \fBsvn diff\fP
.IP \(bu 2
Patch files
.UNINDENT
.sp
Example invocations:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# Run clang\-tidy on all changes in the working directory
$ git diff \-U0 \-\-no\-color HEAD^ | clang\-tidy\-diff.py \-p1

# Run with specific checks and apply fixes
$ git diff \-U0 \-\-no\-color HEAD^ | clang\-tidy\-diff.py \-p1 \-fix \e
  \-checks=\-*,readability\-*

# Run on staged changes
$ git diff \-U0 \-\-no\-color \-\-cached | clang\-tidy\-diff.py \-p1

# Run on changes between two commits
$ git diff \-U0 \-\-no\-color HEAD~2 HEAD | clang\-tidy\-diff.py \-p1

# Run on a patch file
$ clang\-tidy\-diff.py \-p1 < changes.patch
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fB\-p1\fP option tells the script to strip one level of path prefix from
the diff, which is typically needed for Git diffs. The script supports most of
the same options as \fBclang\-tidy\fP itself, including \fB\-checks=\fP,
\fB\-fix\fP, \fB\-header\-filter=\fP, and configuration options.
.sp
While \fBclang\-tidy\-diff.py\fP is useful for focusing on recent changes,
relying solely on it may lead to incomplete analysis. Since the script only
reports warnings from the modified lines, it may miss issues that are caused
by the changes but manifest elsewhere in the code. For example, changes that
only add lines to a function may cause it to violate size limits (e.g.,
readability\-function\-size \%<checks/\:readability/\:function-size\:.html>), but the
diagnostic will be reported at the function declaration, which may not be in
the diff and thus filtered out. Modifications to header files may also affect
many implementation files, but only warnings in the modified header lines will
be reported.
.sp
For comprehensive analysis, especially before merging significant changes,
consider running \fBclang\-tidy\fP on the entire affected files or the
whole project using \fBrun\-clang\-tidy.py\fP\&.
.SS Suppressing Undesired Diagnostics
.sp
\fBclang\-tidy\fP diagnostics are intended to call out code that does not
adhere to a coding standard, or is otherwise problematic in some way. However,
if the code is known to be correct, it may be useful to silence the warning.
Some clang\-tidy checks provide a check\-specific way to silence the diagnostics,
e.g. bugprone\-use\-after\-move \%<checks/\:bugprone/\:use-after-move\:.html> can be
silenced by re\-initializing the variable after it has been moved out,
bugprone\-string\-integer\-assignment \%<checks/\:bugprone/\:string-integer-assignment\:.html> can be suppressed by
explicitly casting the integer to \fBchar\fP,
readability\-implicit\-bool\-conversion \%<checks/\:readability/\:implicit-bool-conversion\:.html> can also be suppressed by
using explicit casts, etc.
.sp
If a specific suppression mechanism is not available for a certain warning, or
its use is not desired for some reason, \fBclang\-tidy\fP has a generic
mechanism to suppress diagnostics using \fBNOLINT\fP, \fBNOLINTNEXTLINE\fP, and
\fBNOLINTBEGIN\fP ... \fBNOLINTEND\fP comments.
.sp
The \fBNOLINT\fP comment instructs \fBclang\-tidy\fP to ignore warnings on
the \fIsame line\fP (it doesn\(aqt apply to a function, a block of code or any other
language construct; it applies to the line of code it is on). If introducing
the comment on the same line would change the formatting in an undesired way,
the \fBNOLINTNEXTLINE\fP comment allows suppressing clang\-tidy warnings on the
\fInext line\fP\&. The \fBNOLINTBEGIN\fP and \fBNOLINTEND\fP comments allow suppressing
clang\-tidy warnings on \fImultiple lines\fP (affecting all lines between the two
comments).
.sp
All comments can be followed by an optional list of check names in parentheses
(see below for the formal syntax). The list of check names supports globbing,
with the same format and semantics as for enabling checks. Note: negative globs
are ignored here, as they would effectively re\-activate the warning.
.sp
For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
class Foo {
  // Suppress all the diagnostics for the line
  Foo(int param); // NOLINT

  // Consider explaining the motivation to suppress the warning
  Foo(char param); // NOLINT: Allow implicit conversion from \(gachar\(ga, because <some valid reason>

  // Silence only the specified checks for the line
  Foo(double param); // NOLINT(google\-explicit\-constructor, google\-runtime\-int)

  // Silence all checks from the \(gagoogle\(ga module
  Foo(bool param); // NOLINT(google*)

  // Silence all checks ending with \(ga\-avoid\-c\-arrays\(ga
  int array[10]; // NOLINT(*\-avoid\-c\-arrays)

  // Silence only the specified diagnostics for the next line
  // NOLINTNEXTLINE(google\-explicit\-constructor, google\-runtime\-int)
  Foo(bool param);

  // Silence all checks from the \(gagoogle\(ga module for the next line
  // NOLINTNEXTLINE(google*)
  Foo(bool param);

  // Silence all checks ending with \(ga\-avoid\-c\-arrays\(ga for the next line
  // NOLINTNEXTLINE(*\-avoid\-c\-arrays)
  int array[10];

  // Silence only the specified checks for all lines between the BEGIN and END
  // NOLINTBEGIN(google\-explicit\-constructor, google\-runtime\-int)
  Foo(short param);
  Foo(long param);
  // NOLINTEND(google\-explicit\-constructor, google\-runtime\-int)

  // Silence all checks from the \(gagoogle\(ga module for all lines between the BEGIN and END
  // NOLINTBEGIN(google*)
  Foo(bool param);
  // NOLINTEND(google*)

  // Silence all checks ending with \(ga\-avoid\-c\-arrays\(ga for all lines between the BEGIN and END
  // NOLINTBEGIN(*\-avoid\-c\-arrays)
  int array[10];
  // NOLINTEND(*\-avoid\-c\-arrays)
};
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The formal syntax of \fBNOLINT\fP, \fBNOLINTNEXTLINE\fP, and \fBNOLINTBEGIN\fP ...
\fBNOLINTEND\fP is the following:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
lint\-comment:
  lint\-command
  lint\-command lint\-args

lint\-args:
  \fB(\fP check\-name\-list \fB)\fP

check\-name\-list:
  \fIcheck\-name\fP
  check\-name\-list \fB,\fP \fIcheck\-name\fP

lint\-command:
  \fBNOLINT\fP
  \fBNOLINTNEXTLINE\fP
  \fBNOLINTBEGIN\fP
  \fBNOLINTEND\fP
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that whitespaces between
\fBNOLINT\fP/\fBNOLINTNEXTLINE\fP/\fBNOLINTBEGIN\fP/\fBNOLINTEND\fP and the opening
parenthesis are not allowed (in this case the comment will be treated just as
\fBNOLINT\fP/\fBNOLINTNEXTLINE\fP/\fBNOLINTBEGIN\fP/\fBNOLINTEND\fP), whereas in the
check names list (inside the parentheses), whitespaces can be used and will be
ignored.
.sp
All \fBNOLINTBEGIN\fP comments must be paired by an equal number of \fBNOLINTEND\fP
comments. Moreover, a pair of comments must have matching arguments \-\- for
example, \fBNOLINTBEGIN(check\-name)\fP can be paired with
\fBNOLINTEND(check\-name)\fP but not with \fBNOLINTEND\fP \fI(zero arguments)\fP\&.
\fBclang\-tidy\fP will generate a \fBclang\-tidy\-nolint\fP error diagnostic if
any \fBNOLINTBEGIN\fP/\fBNOLINTEND\fP comment violates these requirements.
.SH CLANG-INCLUDE-FIXER
.SS Contents
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Clang\-Include\-Fixer
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
Setup
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
Creating a Symbol Index From a Compilation Database
.IP \(bu 2
Integrate with Vim
.IP \(bu 2
Integrate with Emacs
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
How it Works
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
One of the major nuisances of C++ compared to other languages is the manual
management of \fB#include\fP directives in any file.
\fBclang\-include\-fixer\fP addresses one aspect of this problem by providing
an automated way of adding \fB#include\fP directives for missing symbols in one
translation unit.
.sp
While inserting missing \fB#include\fP, \fBclang\-include\-fixer\fP adds
missing namespace qualifiers to all instances of an unidentified symbol if
the symbol is missing some prefix namespace qualifiers.
.SS Setup
.sp
To use \fBclang\-include\-fixer\fP two databases are required. Both can be
generated with existing tools.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Compilation database. Contains the compiler commands for any given file in a
project and can be generated by CMake, see How To Setup Tooling For LLVM \%<https://\:clang\:.llvm\:.org/\:docs/\:HowToSetupToolingForLLVM\:.html>\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Symbol index. Contains all symbol information in a project to match a given
identifier to a header file.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Ideally both databases (\fBcompile_commands.json\fP and
\fBfind_all_symbols_db.yaml\fP) are linked into the root of the source tree they
correspond to. Then the \fBclang\-include\-fixer\fP can automatically pick
them up if called with a source file from that tree. Note that by default
\fBcompile_commands.json\fP as generated by CMake does not include header files,
so only implementation files can be handled by tools.
.SS Creating a Symbol Index From a Compilation Database
.sp
The include fixer contains \fBfind\-all\-symbols\fP, a tool to create a
symbol database in YAML format from a compilation database by parsing all
source files listed in it. The following list of commands shows how to set up a
database for LLVM, any project built by CMake should follow similar steps.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ cd path/to/llvm\-build
$ ninja find\-all\-symbols // build find\-all\-symbols tool.
$ ninja clang\-include\-fixer // build clang\-include\-fixer tool.
$ ls compile_commands.json # Make sure compile_commands.json exists.
  compile_commands.json
$ path/to/llvm/source/clang\-tools\-extra/clang\-include\-fixer/find\-all\-symbols/tool/run\-find\-all\-symbols.py
  ... wait as clang indexes the code base ...
$ ln \-s $PWD/find_all_symbols_db.yaml path/to/llvm/source/ # Link database into the source tree.
$ ln \-s $PWD/compile_commands.json path/to/llvm/source/ # Also link compilation database if it\(aqs not there already.
$ cd path/to/llvm/source
$ /path/to/clang\-include\-fixer \-db=yaml path/to/file/with/missing/include.cpp
  Added #include \(dqfoo.h\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Integrate with Vim
.sp
To run \fIclang\-include\-fixer\fP on a potentially unsaved buffer in Vim. Add the
following key binding to your \fB\&.vimrc\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
noremap <leader>cf :pyf path/to/llvm/source/clang\-tools\-extra/clang\-include\-fixer/tool/clang\-include\-fixer.py<cr>
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This enables \fIclang\-include\-fixer\fP for NORMAL and VISUAL mode. Change
\fI<leader>cf\fP to another binding if you need clang\-include\-fixer on a different
key. The <leader> key \%<http://\:vim\:.wikia\:.com/\:wiki/\:Mapping_keys_in_Vim_-_Tutorial_(Part_3)#\:Map_leader>
is a reference to a specific key defined by the mapleader variable and is bound
to backslash by default.
.sp
Make sure vim can find \fBclang\-include\-fixer\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Add the path to \fBclang\-include\-fixer\fP to the PATH environment variable.
.IP \(bu 2
Or set \fBg:clang_include_fixer_path\fP in vimrc: \fBlet g:clang_include_fixer_path=path/to/clang\-include\-fixer\fP
.UNINDENT
.sp
You can customize the number of headers being shown by setting
\fBlet g:clang_include_fixer_maximum_suggested_headers=5\fP
.sp
Customized settings in \fI\&.vimrc\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlet g:clang_include_fixer_path = \(dqclang\-include\-fixer\(dq\fP
.sp
Set clang\-include\-fixer binary file path.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlet g:clang_include_fixer_maximum_suggested_headers = 3\fP
.sp
Set the maximum number of \fB#includes\fP to show. Default is 3.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlet g:clang_include_fixer_increment_num = 5\fP
.sp
Set the increment number of #includes to show every time when pressing \fBm\fP\&.
Default is 5.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlet g:clang_include_fixer_jump_to_include = 0\fP
.sp
Set to 1 if you want to jump to the new inserted \fB#include\fP line. Default is
0.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlet g:clang_include_fixer_query_mode = 0\fP
.sp
Set to 1 if you want to insert \fB#include\fP for the symbol under the cursor.
Default is 0. Compared to normal mode, this mode won\(aqt parse the source file
and only search the symbol from database, which is faster than normal mode.
.UNINDENT
.sp
See \fBclang\-include\-fixer.py\fP for more details.
.SS Integrate with Emacs
.sp
To run \fIclang\-include\-fixer\fP on a potentially unsaved buffer in Emacs.
Ensure that Emacs finds \fBclang\-include\-fixer.el\fP by adding the directory
containing the file to the \fBload\-path\fP and requiring the \fIclang\-include\-fixer\fP
in your \fB\&.emacs\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
(add\-to\-list \(aqload\-path \(dqpath/to/llvm/source/clang\-tools\-extra/clang\-include\-fixer/tool/\(dq
(require \(aqclang\-include\-fixer)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Within Emacs the tool can be invoked with the command
\fBM\-x clang\-include\-fixer\fP\&. This will insert the header that defines the
first undefined symbol; if there is more than one header that would define the
symbol, the user is prompted to select one.
.sp
To include the header that defines the symbol at point, run
\fBM\-x clang\-include\-fixer\-at\-point\fP\&.
.sp
Make sure Emacs can find \fBclang\-include\-fixer\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Either add the parent directory of \fBclang\-include\-fixer\fP to the PATH
environment variable, or customize the Emacs user option
\fBclang\-include\-fixer\-executable\fP to point to the file name of the program.
.UNINDENT
.SS How it Works
.sp
To get the most information out of Clang at parse time,
\fBclang\-include\-fixer\fP runs in tandem with the parse and receives
callbacks from Clang\(aqs semantic analysis. In particular it reuses the existing
support for typo corrections. Whenever Clang tries to correct a potential typo
it emits a callback to the include fixer which then looks for a corresponding
file. At this point rich lookup information is still available, which is not
available in the AST at a later stage.
.sp
The identifier that should be typo corrected is then sent to the database, if a
header file is returned it is added as an include directive at the top of the
file.
.sp
Currently \fBclang\-include\-fixer\fP only inserts a single include at a
time to avoid getting caught in follow\-up errors. If multiple \fI#include\fP
additions are desired the program can be rerun until a fix\-point is reached.
.SH CLANG-CHANGE-NAMESPACE
.SS Contents
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Clang\-Change\-Namespace
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
Example usage
.IP \(bu 2
Another example
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
Caveats
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
Content already exists in new namespace
.IP \(bu 2
Inline namespace doesn\(aqt work
.IP \(bu 2
Symbol references not updated
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclang\-change\-namespace\fP Command Line Options
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBclang\-change\-namespace\fP can be used to change the surrounding
namespaces of class/function definitions.
.sp
Classes/functions in the moved namespace will have new namespaces while
references to symbols (e.g. types, functions) which are not defined in the
changed namespace will be correctly qualified by prepending namespace specifiers
before them. This will try to add shortest namespace specifiers possible.
.sp
When a symbol reference needs to be fully\-qualified, this adds a \fI::\fP prefix to
the namespace specifiers unless the new namespace is the global namespace. For
classes, only classes that are declared/defined in the given namespace in
specified files will be moved: forward declarations will remain in the old
namespace. The will be demonstrated in the next example.
.SS Example usage
.sp
For example, consider this \fItest.cc\fP example here with the forward declared
class \fIFWD\fP and the defined class \fIA\fP, both in the namespace \fIa\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
namespace a {
class FWD;
class A {
  FWD *fwd;
};
} // namespace a
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
And now let\(aqs change the namespace \fIa\fP to \fIx\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
clang\-change\-namespace \e
  \-\-old_namespace \(dqa\(dq \e
  \-\-new_namespace \(dqx\(dq \e
  \-\-file_pattern \(dqtest.cc\(dq \e
  \-\-i \e
  test.cc
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that in the code below there\(aqs still the forward decalred class \fIFWD\fP that
stayed in the namespace \fIa\fP\&. It wasn\(aqt moved to the new namespace because it
wasn\(aqt defined/declared here in \fIa\fP but only forward declared.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
namespace a {
class FWD;
} // namespace a
namespace x {

class A {
  a::FWD *fwd;
};
} // namespace x
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Another example
.sp
Consider this \fItest.cc\fP file:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
namespace na {
class X {};
namespace nb {
class Y {
  X x;
};
} // namespace nb
} // namespace na
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
To move the definition of class \fIY\fP from namespace \fIna::nb\fP to \fIx::y\fP, run:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
clang\-change\-namespace \e
  \-\-old_namespace \(dqna::nb\(dq \e
  \-\-new_namespace \(dqx::y\(dq \e
  \-\-file_pattern \(dqtest.cc\(dq \e
  \-\-i \e
  test.cc
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This will overwrite \fItest.cc\fP to look like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
namespace na {
class X {};

} // namespace na
namespace x {
namespace y {
class Y {
  na::X x;
};
} // namespace y
} // namespace x
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note, that we\(aqve successfully moved the class \fIY\fP from namespace \fIna::nb\fP to
namespace \fIx::y\fP\&.
.SS Caveats
.SS Content already exists in new namespace
.sp
Consider this \fItest.cc\fP example that defines two \fIclass A\fP one inside the
namespace \fIa\fP and one in namespace \fIb\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
namespace a {
class A {
    int classAFromWithinNamespace_a;
};
} // namespace a

namespace b {
class A {
    int classAFromWithinNamespace_b;
};
} //namespace b
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Let\(aqs move everything from namespace \fIa\fP to namespace \fIb\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
clang\-change\-namespace \e
  \-\-old_namespace \(dqa\(dq \e
  \-\-new_namespace \(dqb\(dq \e
  \-\-file_pattern test.cc \e
  test.cc
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
As expected we now have to definitions of \fIclass A\fP inside the namespace \fIb\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
namespace b {
class A {
  int classAFromWithinNamespace_a;
};
} // namespace b

namespace b {
class A {
    int classAFromWithinNamespace_b;
};
} //namespace b
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The re\-factoring looks correct but the code will not compile due to the name
duplication. It is not up to the tool to ensure compilability in that sense.
But one has to be aware of that.
.SS Inline namespace doesn\(aqt work
.sp
Consider this usage of two versions of implementations for a \fIgreet\fP function:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#include <cstdio>

namespace Greeter {
inline namespace Version1 {
  const char* greet() { return \(dqHello from version 1!\(dq; }
} // namespace Version1
namespace Version2 {
  const char* greet() { return \(dqHello from version 2!\(dq; }
} // namespace Version2
} // namespace Greeter

int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
  printf(\(dq%s\en\(dq, Greeter::greet());
  return 0;
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note, that currently \fIGreeter::greet()\fP will result in a call to
\fIGreeter::Version1::greet()\fP because that\(aqs the inlined namespace.
.sp
Let\(aqs say you want to move one and make \fIVersion2\fP the default now and remove
the \fIinline\fP from the \fIVersion1\fP\&. First let\(aqs try to turn \fInamespace Version2\fP
into \fIinline namespace Version2\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
clang\-change\-namespace \e
  \-\-old_namespace \(dqGreeter::Version2\(dq \e
  \-\-new_namespace \(dqinline Version2\(dq \e
  \-\-file_pattern main.cc main.cc
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
But this will put the \fIinline\fP keyword in the wrong place resulting in:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#include <cstdio>

namespace Greeter {
inline namespace Version1 {
        const char* greet() { return \(dqHello from version 1!\(dq; }
} // namespace Version1

} // namespace Greeter
namespace inline Greeter {
namespace Version2 {
const char *greet() { return \(dqHello from version 2!\(dq; }
} // namespace Version2
} // namespace inline Greeter

int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
        printf(\(dq%s\en\(dq, Greeter::greet());
        return 0;
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
One cannot use \fI:program:\(gaclang\-change\-namespace\fP to inline a namespace.
.SS Symbol references not updated
.sp
Consider this \fItest.cc\fP file:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
namespace old {
struct foo {};
}  // namespace old

namespace b {
old::foo g_foo;
}  // namespace b
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Notice that namespace \fIb\fP defines a global variable of type \fIold::foo\fP\&. If we
now change the name of the \fIold\fP namespace to \fImodern\fP, the reference will not
be updated:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
clang\-change\-namespace \e
  \-\-old_namespace \(dqold\(dq \e
  \-\-new_namespace \(dqmodern\(dq \e
  \-\-file_pattern test.cc \e
  test.cc
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
namespace modern {
struct foo {};
} // namespace modern

namespace b {
old::foo g_foo;
}  // namespace b
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fIg_foo\fP is still of the no longer existing type \fIold::foo\fP while instead it
should use \fImodern::foo\fP\&.
.sp
Only symbol references in the moved namespace are updated, not outside of it.
.SS \fBclang\-change\-namespace\fP Command Line Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-allowed_file=<string>
A file containing regexes of symbol names that are not expected to be updated
when changing namespaces around them.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-dump_result
Dump new file contents in YAML, if specified.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-extra\-arg=<string>
Additional argument to append to the compiler command line
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-extra\-arg\-before=<string>
Additional argument to prepend to the compiler command line
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-file_pattern=<string>
Only rename namespaces in files that match the given regular expression
pattern.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-i
Inplace edit <file>s, if specified.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-new_namespace=<string>
New namespace. Use \fI\(dq\(dq\fP when you target the global namespace.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-old_namespace=<string>
Old namespace.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-p <string>
Build path
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-\-style=<string>
The style name used for reformatting.
.UNINDENT
.SH MODULARIZE USER'S MANUAL
.SS Modularize Usage
.sp
\fBmodularize [<modularize\-options>] [<module\-map>|<include\-files\-list>]*
[<front\-end\-options>...]\fP
.sp
\fB<modularize\-options>\fP is a place\-holder for options
specific to modularize, which are described below in
\fIModularize Command Line Options\fP\&.
.sp
\fB<module\-map>\fP specifies the path of a file name for an
existing module map. The module map must be well\-formed in
terms of syntax. Modularize will extract the header file names
from the map. Only normal headers are checked, assuming headers
marked \(dqprivate\(dq, \(dqtextual\(dq, or \(dqexclude\(dq are not to be checked
as a top\-level include, assuming they either are included by
other headers which are checked, or they are not suitable for
modules.
.sp
\fB<include\-files\-list>\fP specifies the path of a file name for a
file containing the newline\-separated list of headers to check
with respect to each other. Lines beginning with \(aq#\(aq and empty
lines are ignored. Header file names followed by a colon and
other space\-separated file names will include those extra files
as dependencies. The file names can be relative or full paths,
but must be on the same line. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
header1.h
header2.h
header3.h: header1.h header2.h
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that unless a \fB\-prefix (header path)\fP option is specified,
non\-absolute file paths in the header list file will be relative
to the header list file directory. Use \-prefix to specify a different
directory.
.sp
\fB<front\-end\-options>\fP is a place\-holder for regular Clang
front\-end arguments, which must follow the <include\-files\-list>.
Note that by default, modularize assumes .h files
contain C++ source, so if you are using a different language,
you might need to use a \fB\-x\fP option to tell Clang that the
header contains another language, i.e.:  \fB\-x c\fP
.sp
Note also that because modularize does not use the clang driver,
you will likely need to pass in additional compiler front\-end
arguments to match those passed in by default by the driver.
.SS Modularize Command Line Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-prefix=<header\-path>
Prepend the given path to non\-absolute file paths in the header list file.
By default, headers are assumed to be relative to the header list file
directory. Use \fB\-prefix\fP to specify a different directory.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-module\-map\-path=<module\-map\-path>
Generate a module map and output it to the given file. See the description
in Module Map Generation \%<#\:module-map-generation>\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-problem\-files\-list=<problem\-files\-list\-file\-name>
For use only with module map assistant. Input list of files that
have problems with respect to modules. These will still be
included in the generated module map, but will be marked as
\(dqexcluded\(dq headers.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-root\-module=<root\-name>
Put modules generated by the \-module\-map\-path option in an enclosing
module with the given name. See the description in Module Map Generation \%<#\:module-map-generation>\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-block\-check\-header\-list\-only
Limit the #include\-inside\-extern\-or\-namespace\-block
check to only those headers explicitly listed in the header list.
This is a work\-around for avoiding error messages for private includes that
purposefully get included inside blocks.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-no\-coverage\-check
Don\(aqt do the coverage check for a module map.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-coverage\-check\-only
Only do the coverage check for a module map.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-display\-file\-lists
Display lists of good files (no compile errors), problem files,
and a combined list with problem files preceded by a \(aq#\(aq.
This can be used to quickly determine which files have problems.
The latter combined list might be useful in starting to modularize
a set of headers. You can start with a full list of headers,
use \-display\-file\-lists option, and then use the combined list as
your intermediate list, uncommenting\-out headers as you fix them.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBmodularize\fP is a standalone tool that checks whether a set of headers
provides the consistent definitions required to use modules. For example, it
detects whether the same entity (say, a NULL macro or size_t typedef) is
defined in multiple headers or whether a header produces different definitions
under different circumstances. These conditions cause modules built from the
headers to behave poorly, and should be fixed before introducing a module
map.
.sp
\fBmodularize\fP also has an assistant mode option for generating
a module map file based on the provided header list. The generated file
is a functional module map that can be used as a starting point for a
module.modulemap file.
.SS Getting Started
.sp
To build from source:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Read Getting Started with the LLVM System \%<https://\:llvm\:.org/\:docs/\:GettingStarted\:.html> and Clang Tools
Documentation \%<https://\:clang\:.llvm\:.org/\:docs/\:ClangTools\:.html> for information on getting sources for LLVM, Clang, and
Clang Extra Tools.
.IP 2. 3
Getting Started with the LLVM System \%<https://\:llvm\:.org/\:docs/\:GettingStarted\:.html> and Building LLVM with CMake \%<https://\:llvm\:.org/\:docs/\:CMake\:.html> give
directions for how to build. With sources all checked out into the
right place the LLVM build will build Clang Extra Tools and their
dependencies automatically.
.INDENT 3.0
.IP \(bu 2
If using CMake, you can also use the \fBmodularize\fP target to build
just the modularize tool and its dependencies.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Before continuing, take a look at Modularize Usage \%<> to see how to invoke
modularize.
.SS What Modularize Checks
.sp
Modularize will check for the following:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Duplicate global type and variable definitions
.IP \(bu 2
Duplicate macro definitions
.IP \(bu 2
Macro instances, \(aqdefined(macro)\(aq, or #if, #elif, #ifdef, #ifndef conditions
that evaluate differently in a header
.IP \(bu 2
#include directives inside \(aqextern \(dqC/C++\(dq {}\(aq or \(aqnamespace (name) {}\(aq blocks
.IP \(bu 2
Module map header coverage completeness (in the case of a module map input
only)
.UNINDENT
.sp
Modularize will do normal C/C++ parsing, reporting normal errors and warnings,
but will also report special error messages like the following:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
error: \(aq(symbol)\(aq defined at multiple locations:
   (file):(row):(column)
   (file):(row):(column)

error: header \(aq(file)\(aq has different contents depending on how it was included
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The latter might be followed by messages like the following:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
note: \(aq(symbol)\(aq in (file) at (row):(column) not always provided
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Checks will also be performed for macro expansions, defined(macro)
expressions, and preprocessor conditional directives that evaluate
inconsistently, and can produce error messages like the following:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
 (...)/SubHeader.h:11:5:
#if SYMBOL == 1
    ^
error: Macro instance \(aqSYMBOL\(aq has different values in this header,
       depending on how it was included.
  \(aqSYMBOL\(aq expanded to: \(aq1\(aq with respect to these inclusion paths:
    (...)/Header1.h
      (...)/SubHeader.h
(...)/SubHeader.h:3:9:
#define SYMBOL 1
        ^
Macro defined here.
  \(aqSYMBOL\(aq expanded to: \(aq2\(aq with respect to these inclusion paths:
    (...)/Header2.h
        (...)/SubHeader.h
(...)/SubHeader.h:7:9:
#define SYMBOL 2
        ^
Macro defined here.
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Checks will also be performed for \(aq#include\(aq directives that are
nested inside \(aqextern \(dqC/C++\(dq {}\(aq or \(aqnamespace (name) {}\(aq blocks,
and can produce error message like the following:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
IncludeInExtern.h:2:3:
#include \(dqEmpty.h\(dq
^
error: Include directive within extern \(dqC\(dq {}.
IncludeInExtern.h:1:1:
extern \(dqC\(dq {
^
The \(dqextern \(dqC\(dq {}\(dq block is here.
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Module Map Coverage Check
.sp
The coverage check uses the Clang library to read and parse the
module map file. Starting at the module map file directory, or just the
include paths, if specified, it will collect the names of all the files it
considers headers (no extension, .h, or .inc\-\-if you need more, modify the
isHeader function). It then compares the headers against those referenced
in the module map, either explicitly named, or implicitly named via an
umbrella directory or umbrella file, as parsed by the ModuleMap object.
If headers are found which are not referenced or covered by an umbrella
directory or file, warning messages will be produced, and this program
will return an error code of 1. If no problems are found, an error code of
0 is returned.
.sp
Note that in the case of umbrella headers, this tool invokes the compiler
to preprocess the file, and uses a callback to collect the header files
included by the umbrella header or any of its nested includes. If any
front end options are needed for these compiler invocations, these
can be included on the command line after the module map file argument.
.sp
Warning message have the form:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
warning: module.modulemap does not account for file: Level3A.h
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that for the case of the module map referencing a file that does
not exist, the module map parser in Clang will (at the time of this
writing) display an error message.
.sp
To limit the checks \fBmodularize\fP does to just the module
map coverage check, use the \fB\-coverage\-check\-only option\fP\&.
.sp
For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
modularize \-coverage\-check\-only module.modulemap
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Module Map Generation
.sp
If you specify the \fB\-module\-map\-path=<module map file>\fP,
\fBmodularize\fP will output a module map based on the input header list.
A module will be created for each header. Also, if the header in the header
list is a partial path, a nested module hierarchy will be created in which a
module will be created for each subdirectory component in the header path,
with the header itself represented by the innermost module. If other headers
use the same subdirectories, they will be enclosed in these same modules also.
.sp
For example, for the header list:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
SomeTypes.h
SomeDecls.h
SubModule1/Header1.h
SubModule1/Header2.h
SubModule2/Header3.h
SubModule2/Header4.h
SubModule2.h
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following module map will be generated:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
// Output/NoProblemsAssistant.txt
// Generated by: modularize \-module\-map\-path=Output/NoProblemsAssistant.txt \e
     \-root\-module=Root NoProblemsAssistant.modularize

module SomeTypes {
  header \(dqSomeTypes.h\(dq
  export *
}
module SomeDecls {
  header \(dqSomeDecls.h\(dq
  export *
}
module SubModule1 {
  module Header1 {
    header \(dqSubModule1/Header1.h\(dq
    export *
  }
  module Header2 {
    header \(dqSubModule1/Header2.h\(dq
    export *
  }
}
module SubModule2 {
  module Header3 {
    header \(dqSubModule2/Header3.h\(dq
    export *
  }
  module Header4 {
    header \(dqSubModule2/Header4.h\(dq
    export *
  }
  header \(dqSubModule2.h\(dq
  export *
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An optional \fB\-root\-module=<root\-name>\fP option can be used to cause a root module
to be created which encloses all the modules.
.sp
An optional \fB\-problem\-files\-list=<problem\-file\-name>\fP can be used to input
a list of files to be excluded, perhaps as a temporary stop\-gap measure until
problem headers can be fixed.
.sp
For example, with the same header list from above:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
// Output/NoProblemsAssistant.txt
// Generated by: modularize \-module\-map\-path=Output/NoProblemsAssistant.txt \e
     \-root\-module=Root NoProblemsAssistant.modularize

module Root {
  module SomeTypes {
    header \(dqSomeTypes.h\(dq
    export *
  }
  module SomeDecls {
    header \(dqSomeDecls.h\(dq
    export *
  }
  module SubModule1 {
    module Header1 {
      header \(dqSubModule1/Header1.h\(dq
      export *
    }
    module Header2 {
      header \(dqSubModule1/Header2.h\(dq
      export *
    }
  }
  module SubModule2 {
    module Header3 {
      header \(dqSubModule2/Header3.h\(dq
      export *
    }
    module Header4 {
      header \(dqSubModule2/Header4.h\(dq
      export *
    }
    header \(dqSubModule2.h\(dq
    export *
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that headers with dependents will be ignored with a warning, as the
Clang module mechanism doesn\(aqt support headers that rely on other headers
being included first.
.sp
The module map format defines some keywords which can\(aqt be used in module
names. If a header has one of these names, an underscore (\(aq_\(aq) will be
prepended to the name. For example, if the header name is \fBheader.h\fP,
because \fBheader\fP is a keyword, the module name will be \fB_header\fP\&.
For a list of the module map keywords, please see:
Lexical structure \%<https://\:clang\:.llvm\:.org/\:docs/\:Modules\:.html#\:lexical-structure>
.SH PP-TRACE USER'S MANUAL
.sp
\fBpp\-trace\fP is a standalone tool that traces preprocessor
activity. It\(aqs also used as a test of Clang\(aqs PPCallbacks interface.
It runs a given source file through the Clang preprocessor, displaying
selected information from callback functions overridden in a
PPCallbacks \%<https://\:clang\:.llvm\:.org/\:doxygen/\:classclang_1_1PPCallbacks\:.html>
derivation. The output is in a high\-level YAML format, described in
pp\-trace Output Format\&.
.SS pp\-trace Usage
.SS Command Line Format
.sp
\fBpp\-trace [<pp\-trace\-options>] <source\-file> [\-\- <front\-end\-options>]\fP
.sp
\fB<pp\-trace\-options>\fP is a place\-holder for options
specific to pp\-trace, which are described below in
Command Line Options\&.
.sp
\fB<source\-file>\fP specifies the source file to run through the preprocessor.
.sp
\fB<front\-end\-options>\fP is a place\-holder for regular
Clang Compiler Options \%<https://\:clang\:.llvm\:.org/\:docs/\:UsersManual\:.html#\:command-line-options>,
which must follow the <source\-file>.
.SS Command Line Options
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-callbacks <comma\-separated\-globs>
This option specifies a comma\-separated list of globs describing the list of
callbacks that should be traced. Globs are processed in order of appearance.
Positive globs add matched callbacks to the set, negative globs (those with
the \(aq\-\(aq prefix) remove callacks from the set.
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
FileChanged
.IP \(bu 2
FileSkipped
.IP \(bu 2
InclusionDirective
.IP \(bu 2
moduleImport
.IP \(bu 2
EndOfMainFile
.IP \(bu 2
Ident
.IP \(bu 2
PragmaDirective
.IP \(bu 2
PragmaComment
.IP \(bu 2
PragmaDetectMismatch
.IP \(bu 2
PragmaDebug
.IP \(bu 2
PragmaMessage
.IP \(bu 2
PragmaDiagnosticPush
.IP \(bu 2
PragmaDiagnosticPop
.IP \(bu 2
PragmaDiagnostic
.IP \(bu 2
PragmaOpenCLExtension
.IP \(bu 2
PragmaWarning
.IP \(bu 2
PragmaWarningPush
.IP \(bu 2
PragmaWarningPop
.IP \(bu 2
MacroExpands
.IP \(bu 2
MacroDefined
.IP \(bu 2
MacroUndefined
.IP \(bu 2
Defined
.IP \(bu 2
SourceRangeSkipped
.IP \(bu 2
If
.IP \(bu 2
Elif
.IP \(bu 2
Ifdef
.IP \(bu 2
Ifndef
.IP \(bu 2
Else
.IP \(bu 2
Endif
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \-output <output\-file>
By default, pp\-trace outputs the trace information to stdout. Use this
option to output the trace information to a file.
.UNINDENT
.SS pp\-trace Output Format
.sp
The pp\-trace output is formatted as YAML. See \%<https://\:yaml\:.org> for general
YAML information. It\(aqs arranged as a sequence of information about the
callback call, including the callback name and argument information, for
example::
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\-\-\-
\- Callback: Name
  Argument1: Value1
  Argument2: Value2
(etc.)
\&...
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
With real data::
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\-\-\-
\- Callback: FileChanged
  Loc: \(dqc:/Clang/llvm/clang\-tools\-extra/test/pp\-trace/pp\-trace\-include.cpp:1:1\(dq
  Reason: EnterFile
  FileType: C_User
  PrevFID: (invalid)
  (etc.)
\- Callback: FileChanged
  Loc: \(dqD:/Clang/llvm/clang\-tools\-extra/test/pp\-trace/pp\-trace\-include.cpp:5:1\(dq
  Reason: ExitFile
  FileType: C_User
  PrevFID: \(dqD:/Clang/llvm/clang\-tools\-extra/test/pp\-trace/Input/Level1B.h\(dq
\- Callback: EndOfMainFile
\&...
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In all but one case (MacroDirective) the \(dqArgument\(dq scalars have the same
name as the argument in the corresponding PPCallbacks callback function.
.SS Callback Details
.sp
The following sections describe the purpose and output format for each callback.
.sp
Click on the callback name in the section heading to see the Doxygen
documentation for the callback.
.sp
The argument descriptions table describes the callback argument information
displayed.
.sp
The Argument Name field in most (but not all) cases is the same name as the
callback function parameter.
.sp
The Argument Value Syntax field describes the values that will be displayed
for the argument value. It uses an ad hoc representation that mixes literal
and symbolic representations. Enumeration member symbols are shown as the
actual enum member in a (member1|member2|...) form. A name in parentheses
can either represent a place holder for the described value, or confusingly,
it might be a literal, such as (null), for a null pointer.
Locations are shown as quoted only to avoid confusing the documentation generator.
.sp
The Clang C++ Type field is the type from the callback function declaration.
.sp
The description describes the argument or what is displayed for it.
.sp
Note that in some cases, such as when a structure pointer is an argument
value, only some key member or members are shown to represent the value,
instead of trying to display all members of the structure.
.SS FileChanged \%<https://\:clang\:.llvm\:.org/\:doxygen/\:classclang_1_1PPCallbacks\:.html#\:a7cc8cfaf34114fc65e92af621cd6464e> Callback
.sp
FileChanged is called when the preprocessor enters or exits a file, both the
top level file being compiled, as well as any #include directives. It will
also be called as a result of a system header pragma or in internal renaming
of a file.
.sp
Argument descriptions:
.TS
box center;
l|l|l|l.
T{
Argument Name
T}	T{
Argument Value Syntax
T}	T{
Clang C++ Type
T}	T{
Description
T}
_
T{
Loc
T}	T{
\(dq(file):(line):(col)\(dq
T}	T{
SourceLocation
T}	T{
The location of the directive.
T}
_
T{
Reason
T}	T{
(EnterFile|ExitFile|SystemHeaderPragma|RenameFile)
T}	T{
PPCallbacks::FileChangeReason
T}	T{
Reason for change.
T}
_
T{
FileType
T}	T{
(C_User|C_System|C_ExternCSystem)
T}	T{
SrcMgr::CharacteristicKind
T}	T{
Include type.
T}
_
T{
PrevFID
T}	T{
((file)|(invalid))
T}	T{
FileID
T}	T{
Previous file, if any.
T}
.TE
.sp
Example::
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\- Callback: FileChanged
  Loc: \(dqD:/Clang/llvm/clang\-tools\-extra/test/pp\-trace/pp\-trace\-include.cpp:1:1\(dq
  Reason: EnterFile
  FileType: C_User
  PrevFID: (invalid)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
